    77	
    78	# Adversarial Review (General)
    79	
    80	**Goal:** Cynically review content and produce findings.
    81	
    82	**Your Role:** You are a cynical, jaded reviewer with zero patience for sloppy work. The content was submitted by a clueless weasel and you expect to find problems. Be skeptical of everything. Look for what's missing, not just what's wrong. Use a precise, professional tone — no profanity or personal attacks.
    83	
    84	**Inputs:**
    85	- **content** — Content to review: diff, spec, story, doc, or any artifact
    86	- **also_consider** (optional) — Areas to keep in mind during review alongside normal adversarial analysis
    87	
    88	
    89	## EXECUTION
    90	
    91	### Step 1: Receive Content
    92	
    93	- Load the content to review from provided input or context
    94	- If content to review is empty, ask for clarification and abort
    95	- Identify content type (diff, branch, uncommitted changes, document, etc.)
    96	
    97	### Step 2: Adversarial Analysis
    98	
    99	Review with extreme skepticism — assume problems exist. Find at least ten issues to fix or improve in the provided content.
   100	
   101	### Step 3: Present Findings
   102	
   103	Output findings as a Markdown list (descriptions only).
   104	
   105	
   106	## HALT CONDITIONS
   107	
   108	- HALT if zero findings — this is suspicious, re-analyze or ask for guidance
   109	- HALT if content is empty or unreadable
   110	
   111	--- DESIGN ---
   112	# Trace detection on integer arrays
   113	
   114	**Why now.** After the scoring gate was fixed (parallel arrays, 2026-09-03) the window loop is
   115	**90.9% trace detection** and 4.8% scoring. Everything else is rounding. Tracing is the tool.
   116	
   117	**What it runs on today.** `materializeWindow()` rebuilds an OpenMS `PeakMap` from the compact store:
   118	`Peak1D` (double m/z + float intensity) plus a parallel `FloatDataArray` of mobility, wrapped in an
   119	`MSSpectrum` per frame -- ~20 B/peak against the store's 10 B, plus per-spectrum object overhead,
   120	and every m/z is a double that was an integer twice over (a TOF bin, then a 1e-5 Da store index).
   121	`MassTraceDetection` then matches by a ppm tolerance on those doubles.
   122	
   123	**Goal.** Reimplement the SAME tracing semantics on integer arrays, with all three axes integer:
   124	
   125	| axis | today | here |
   126	|---|---|---|
   127	| retention time | `double` per peak, per spectrum | `uint32` frame index on the global RT axis |
   128	| m/z | `double`, twice-derived | `uint32` flight-time bin (`TofAxis`) |
   129	| ion mobility | `double` 1/K0, from a `uint16` quantum | `uint16` TIMS scan index |
   130	
   131	## 1. The mobility axis, derived without the vendor table
   132	
   133	Within one frame the distinct 1/K0 values ARE the scans, so ranking them recovers the scan index
   134	exactly: collect a frame's distinct mobility values (about a thousand), sort descending (1/K0 falls
   135	as scan number rises), and a peak's rank is its scan. No TIMS calibration table is needed to say
   136	that two peaks came from the same scan -- and comparing scans is what the gate actually wants,
   137	because the calibrated value drifts frame to frame while the scan does not.
   138	
   139	Tolerance conversion: `gate:delta_im` in 1/K0 becomes a scan count per frame, from that frame's own
   140	ranked values. It is not a constant across frames, and pretending it is would be the same mistake as
   141	a fixed ppm halo.
   142	
   143	## 2. The data structure
   144	
   145	```cpp
   146	struct PeakSlab                    // one window, frame-major, 10 B/peak, no OpenMS objects
   147	{
   148	  vector<uint32_t> frame_off;      // F+1: frame f owns [frame_off[f], frame_off[f+1])
   149	  vector<uint32_t> rt_index;       // F: frame -> global RT axis
   150	  vector<double>   b;              // F: calibration factor, for export only
   151	  vector<uint32_t> tof;            // ascending within a frame
   152	  vector<float>    inten;
   153	  vector<uint16_t> scan;           // TIMS scan index
   154	};
   155	```
   156	
   157	Ascending `tof` within a frame is what makes extension a binary search over ~50k values rather than
   158	a scan, and it comes free: the loader already sorts by m/z, which is monotone in TOF.
   159	
   160	## 3. The algorithm, kept faithful to the current one
   161	
   162	`MassTraceDetection` is: seed on the most intense unused peak; extend in both RT directions taking
   163	the closest unused peak within the mass tolerance of a RUNNING centroid; stop a direction when the
   164	hit rate over visited frames falls below `min_sample_rate`; emit if the length is in range. That is
   165	what is reimplemented -- not a new algorithm -- so the comparison is about data structures, not
   166	about science.
   167	
   168	```
   169	  order = peak indices sorted by intensity DESC          // seeding order, as today
   170	  used  = bitset over peaks
   171	  for seed in order:
   172	      if used[seed] or inten[seed] < snr * noise: continue
   173	      centroid = tof[seed]; sum = inten*tof; wsum = inten
   174	      for dir in (+1, -1):
   175	          misses = 0; visited = 0
   176	          for f = frame(seed)+dir, stepping by dir:
   177	              span = tofSpan(centroid, b[f], mass_ppm)        // integer, per frame
   178	              k = closest unused peak in frame f with |tof-centroid| <= span
   179	                                       and |scan - scan(seed)| <= scanTol(f)
   180	              visited++
   181	              if none: misses++; if hits/visited < min_sample_rate: break
   182	              else: take it, update centroid, used[k] = true
   183	      emit if length in [min_trace_length, max_trace_length]
   184	```
   185	
   186	Every inner step is: one binary search in a sorted `uint32` array, then a short linear walk over
   187	candidates in the tolerance window. No doubles are compared, no objects are constructed, and the
   188	peak arrays are touched at 10 B/peak instead of 20 B plus indirection.
   189	
   190	**Valley splitting is NOT reimplemented.** `ElutionPeakDetection` runs afterwards on the traces this
   191	produces, exactly as it does now, so that stage's behaviour is unchanged and out of scope.
   192	
   193	## 4. What this is expected to buy, and what would falsify it
   194	
   195	Trace detection is 3,869 s of 4,256 s of window-elapsed time on dataset D.
   196	
   197	| source of gain | mechanism | estimate |
   198	|---|---|---|
   199	| no `PeakMap` materialisation | 20 B/peak + object overhead -> 10 B/peak, and the conversion loop disappears | memory: −1 to −2 GB per in-flight window |
   200	| integer comparison | `uint32` difference instead of a ppm test on doubles | small; the loop is not ALU-bound |
   201	| **cache** | a frame's peaks are three contiguous arrays instead of `Peak1D` + a parallel array behind an `MSSpectrum` | **the term that should dominate** |
   202	| no double conversion | the store's integers are used as integers | one pass over 1.2e9 peaks removed |
   203	
   204	**Estimate: 30-50% off trace detection, i.e. dataset D from 7:04 to roughly 5:00-6:00**, with peak memory
   205	down by the materialisation. Stated as a range because the split between bandwidth and the
   206	detector's own arithmetic has not been isolated -- the same uncertainty that made me underestimate
   207	the scoring gate by a factor of five, in the conservative direction.
   208	
   209	**Falsifier:** if trace detection does not fall by at least 15%, the cost was never the data
   210	structure and is in the extension search itself -- in which case the lever is the search (bucketing
   211	by tof, or capping candidates per frame), not the layout, and this refactor should be reverted
   212	rather than kept for tidiness.
   213	
   214	**Output.** Faithful, but not bit-identical: the seeding order among equal intensities and the
   215	closest-peak tie-breaks will differ. Judged on identified peptides with both engines.
   216	
   217	## 5. Risks, stated before the review
   218	
   219	1. **`min_sample_rate` semantics.** OpenMS computes the hit rate over the frames it has visited in
   220	   that direction. Reimplementing it subtly differently changes which traces survive, and it is the
   221	   one MS2 trace-validity lever this project has found to matter.
   222	2. **Seeding order.** OpenMS sorts by intensity; ties are broken by whatever the sort does. Ours
   223	   must be deterministic, or runs will not reproduce.
   224	3. **The `used` marking is the parallelism hazard.** It is what makes tracing sequential within a
   225	   band; bands are independent, so the parallel structure is unchanged, but a shared `used` across
   226	   bands would be a race.
   227	4. **The mobility rank assumes a frame's distinct 1/K0 values are its scans.** If a converter has
   228	   already merged or re-binned mobility, the rank is not the scan. This must be checked on real data
   229	   before it is trusted, not assumed.
   230	
