  1457	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:9:The window loop alone consumes 77,333 CPU-s = 21.48 CPU-h—2.94× the reference implementation’s entire 7.3 CPU-h. Even deleting every other speXtractor phase would not produce CPU parity. Scheduler improvements can plausibly remove 15–25% wall time. Closing the remaining gap requires reducing the number of fragment traces and/or precursor–fragment correlation work.
  1458	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:11:There is also a revision mismatch. The measured run is clearly from the parent of commit `d0b3cb4`. The current file has already replaced the rigid 8×12 nested grid with one 100-thread task pool, parallelized `FragGrid`, changed the admission multiplier from 10× to 16×, and shortened the wait from 200 to 20 ms. See [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2322](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2322), [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2344](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2344), and [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2363](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2363). The supplied measurements therefore do not characterize the current source.
  1459	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:17:The per-window stage timers are elapsed stage durations summed across windows—not CPU-seconds, despite the incorrect log message at [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2671](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2671). The timers surround stages with `steady_clock`, for example [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2415](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2415) and [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2472](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2472).
  1460	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:23:- Average cores used per active window:
  1461	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:33:| Known serial stages | at least 4.06 | Materialize + old serial `fraggrid` + emit = 420.9 of 8139.5 window-s, using one rather than 12 cores |
  1462	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:36:The serial-stage estimate is only a lower bound: the `trace` timer also includes serial band construction, sorting, canonicalization and result merging surrounding the parallel detector.
  1463	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:40:- **Eight-window cap:** not the primary limit. Eight windows × twelve threads already exposes 96 threads. The four permanently unused threads cost at most 4.2%. The rigid topology is bad chiefly because idle teams cannot help surviving windows, which the current task-pool rewrite explicitly addresses at [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2363](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2363).
  1464	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:42:- **Across-window imbalance/admission:** measurable but bounded. Perfectly filling eight slots reduces the loop from 1139 to \(8139.5/8 = 1017.4\) s: at most 121.6 s, or 9.0% of total wall time. Because admission waiting is outside the stage timers, the supplied data cannot separate window-size tail from memory admission.
  1465	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:45:  \((395.8-395.8/12)/8 ≈ 45\) wall-s, about 3.3% of total runtime. The current implementation now uses a task loop at [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:741](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:741).
  1466	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:47:- **Inside-band/precursor imbalance:** real. After accounting for outer vacancy and the explicitly serial stages, roughly 13.8 of the missing 28.1 cores remain. Quantile bands balance sampled peak count, not `MassTraceDetection` work; its candidate work is locally superlinear in peak density. The partition and detector are at [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:968](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:968) and [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1015](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1015). Scoring also uses chunks of 32 precursors whose costs vary with IM density, support length and emitted-fragment count; current equivalent is [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2560](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2560).
  1467	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:49:- **Raw trace/correlation volume:** the actual binding problem. Trace and score occupy 94.83% of summed active window time. The scoring path takes an IM slice, performs the RT gate, then walks every surviving fragment’s CSR support for the dot product at [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1515](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1515) and [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1534](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1534). With up to 8.5 M fragment traces per window, this is an enormous intermediate workload unrelated to the final count of 863 k spectra.
  1468	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:56:- Exactly eight window workers and real 12-thread nested teams were used.
  1469	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:58:- Admission did not regularly restrict execution below eight windows.
  1470	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:59:- The timers measure elapsed per-window stage duration—as the source shows—rather than CPU time as the log incorrectly claims.
  1471	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:61:The settling measurement is cheap: record, per window and band, admission-wait time, stage start/end, thread CPU time and active-team size. One OMPT trace or even four atomic histograms would separate admission, window tail, band imbalance and memory stalls.
