  1860	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:486:All mechanics confirmed. Now I have everything needed to write the review. Let me also quickly check the ms1 band path (line 2030-2040) and the "auto" default — getIntOption default 12, and auto derives ceil(threads/windows). Also confirm the default of `perf:trace_bands` is 12 (line 909) and `perf:ms1_trace_bands` default 12 (line 908).
  1861	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:490:  Also note: peaks below noise_threshold_int are filtered out before apices (line 243-248). The same peaks would be filtered in each band — fine.
  1862	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:494:  Another subtle exactness break: `sub[b].sortSpectra()` and MTD requiring >= 3 spectra; a band with < 3 spectra emits nothing — but since every spectrum is copied to every band (with peaks possibly empty), a band only has <3 spectra if fewer than 3 spectra had peaks in its range. Edge case: a trace near an edge in a sparse band could be dropped entirely. Minor but worth noting — actually if sub[b].size() < 3, traces whose core is in that band are LOST entirely, even though the unbanded run would find them. That's a real deletion channel, though rare. Also conversely, the unbanded path requires map.size() >= 3; a banded window with map >= 3 but a band < 3 loses those peaks' traces.
  1863	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:498:  Another point for mechanism 2 (drift): since centroid is intensity-weighted mean and each accepted peak is within ±3σ of the centroid, the maximum displacement of the mean per accepted peak is 3σ·I_new/(I_tot+I_new). There's no a priori bound on the total walk. But can we bound it by the trace's m/z extremity? The min/max peak m/z of a trace: each peak is within 3σ of the running centroid at acceptance time, and the centroid is always within the convex hull of previously accepted peaks. So every peak m/z lies within... let's think: centroid_k ∈ hull of peaks 1..k. Peak k+1 ∈ centroid_k ± 3σ_k. So peak k+1 ∈ hull(1..k) ± 3σ. So the trace's total m/z span can grow by at most 3σ per RT step, with σ re-estimated. Unbounded in principle, but practically: trace spans are actually small because real mass traces track one chemical species; but noise peak capture can extend. The point stands: no derivable constant multiple.
  1864	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:501:  - apex at m/z 500.0000, ppm = 20 → initial sd = 500*20e-6 = 0.01, window ±0.03.
  1865	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:502:  - Scans 1..20, each has one peak, intensities large and constant, m/z increasing by 0.029 per scan (within ±0.03 of current centroid... need to be careful: centroid lags the peak series. With equal intensities, centroid after peak k is mean of first k peaks. If peaks increase linearly at step d, the gap between peak k+1 and centroid_k is d·k/2, which exceeds 3σ=0.03 once k > ~2. Hmm — so equal-intensity linear drift can't actually walk far with step just inside tolerance; the mean lags. Let me think again.
  1866	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:504:  To make the centroid walk, you need each new peak within 3σ of the current mean. With equal weights, mean is arithmetic mean; if each successive peak is at most ε above the current mean, then the mean increments by ≤ ε/(k+1) at step k — total walk bounded by ε·H_n ≈ ε·ln(n) with equal intensities. Hmm, that's a bound! With harmonic weights, the sum over k of ε/(k+1) diverges as ε ln n. So for a trace of length L scans, total drift ≤ 3σ·ln(L) roughly with equal intensities. With intensity weighting, a new peak with intensity I_new moves mean by (p - c)·I_new/(I_tot + I_new) ≤ 3σ·I_new/I_tot. If each new peak has intensity ≥ geometric ratio of accumulated sum (e.g., I_{k+1} = I_tot), mean moves 3σ/2 each step — then drift ≤ 1.5σ·L, linear in trace length. So with escalating intensities, walk per step can stay O(σ), giving total span O(σ·L). Unbounded a priori but you need intensity escalation. With approximately constant intensity, span ~3σ·ln L. For L=100 scans, ln100≈4.6 → ~14σ ≈ 4.7·(3σ) = 14 ppm·(mz/500·... whatever). So 20x ppm halo ≈ 6.7·(3σ) — a 100-scan constant-intensity drifting trace would span ~14σ > 6.7σ halo. That's a decent concrete example! Let me construct it carefully.
  1867	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:506:  Setup: mz base 500, mass_error_ppm=20 → ftl_sd initial = 0.01, acceptance window = ±3σ = ±0.03 around centroid. reestimate_mt_sd will adjust σ but assume it stays ~0.01 (robust estimator tracks spread; a monotone walk increases spread, actually increasing σ, making it worse — conservative to ignore).
  1868	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:508:  Equal intensities I=1. Centroid after k peaks = mean of the k accepted m/z values (weighted mean with equal weights). Strategy: at each new scan, place peak at centroid + 0.029 (just inside +3σ = +0.03). Then centroid increments: c_{k+1} = c_k + 0.029/(k+1). So peak p_{k+1} = c_k + 0.029, and c_{k+1} = c_k + 0.029/(k+1).
  1869	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:510:  After L scans: c_L ≈ 0.029·(H_L - 1) ≈ 0.029 ln L. For L = 60: H_60 ≈ 4.68, minus 1 → 3.68 → drift ≈ 0.107 m/z ≈ 10.7σ ≈ 3.6× the 20×ppm halo half-width (halo = 20 ppm × 500 = 0.01·20 = ... wait halo = mz·ppm·1e-6·20 = 500·20e-6·20 = 0.2 m/z). Hmm! Halo half-width at 500 with ppm 20 is 0.2 m/z = 20σ. So drift 0.107 < 0.2. Need longer trace: drift ≈ 0.029 ln L; to exceed 0.2 need ln L > 6.9 → L ≈ 1000 scans. That's a 1000-scan trace — unrealistic but exists in principle (apex walk). Hmm, but wait — reestimate_mt_sd would inflate σ as the walk proceeds, since the spread of points around the mean grows, widening the window beyond 0.03 and accelerating the walk. So the equal-intensity bound with fixed σ underestimates.
  1870	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:514:  Mechanism 3 example (cleanest): two peaks in one spectrum inside the halo region. Trace T with centroid near band edge e. In spectrum s, two candidate peaks within IM tolerance: p1 at mz e-δ1 (in band A's core side) and p2 at e+δ2 (band B's core side), both within window, δ1 > δ2? findBestPeak_ picks the closer to centroid. If the unbanded trace's centroid is closer to p1, it picks p1. That happens identically in the band — the candidate set within ±3σ is the same as long as both peaks are within the band's halo. So nearest-peak selection alone doesn't differ unless a candidate lies OUTSIDE the halo but inside ±3σ — that's the halo-undersize issue (mechanism 1), fixed at 20× if span bounded.
  1871	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:519:  (b) min_sample_rate validity: isTraceValid_ computes trace.size()/adjusted_scans over the peaks gathered — membership differences flip valid/invalid.
  1872	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:521:  (c) EPD valley splitting: operates on gathered intensities; membership differences change split points. Also EPD smooths with SG window — content-dependent.
  1873	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:523:  (d) σ re-estimation: ftl_sd depends on gathered peaks → acceptance window differs.
  1874	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:529:  Now mechanism 2 assessment: is drift really a practical killer of the 20× halo? The walk argument with equal intensities gives ~3σ·ln L which for L=100 is ~14σ ≈ 4.6×(3σ). Halo is 20 ppm = 6.67×3σ at base mz... halo = 20·ppm_abs = 20σ_initial (since σ_initial = ppm_abs). So halo half-width = 20σ vs equal-intensity walk 3σ ln L → exceeds at ln L > 6.67 → L > 800 scans. With σ re-estimation inflating, earlier. With intensity escalation (apex near trace start — common: apex is max intensity), early steps can move the mean fast: first peak after apex with equal intensity moves centroid up to 1.5σ; then decays. Actually let's compute worst case with intensity weighting: step k move ≤ 3σ·I_k/S_k. To maximize total, choose I_k = S_{k-1} (doubling), gives 1.5σ per step: after L steps, 1.5σL. But intensity can't grow forever — data-dependent. Practically, the apex is the most intense peak typically, so I_new/I_tot is small-ish. So drift is real but 20× probably covers most real traces; the honest statement: no bound exists, and σ re-estimation (default true, not overridden — cite line absence + OpenMS line 34 default) makes even the per-step window data-dependent. The measured N-dependence proves the claim false empirically regardless of which mechanism dominates; mechanism 3 alone is sufficient and needs no pathological input.
  1875	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:531:  Also important: the help text claim "Traces cannot span more than trace:mass_error_ppm in m/z" is false even per-step: the per-step window is ±3·ftl_sd = ±3×mass_error_ppm (line 537: ftl_sd = centroid·ppm/1e6; bounds ±3σ in findBestPeak_/isPeakAcceptable_). So even a single step spans 3× ppm, not 1×. The help text's premise is off by 3× before drift is even considered. That's a nice hostile point: the claimed bound isn't even the per-peak tolerance.
  1876	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:535:  (a) Halo from a real bound on trace span: no a priori bound exists (drift + σ re-estimation). You could bound empirically: run once, measure max trace m/z span (max-min peak m/z per trace) at some N, set halo > max span. But span is itself band-dependent — chicken-and-egg. Could do adaptive: detect traces, if any kept trace's extent (min/max member m/z) comes within X of a core edge, widen halo/redo that band. A fixpoint scheme: exact iff no trace crosses a band's halo boundary... but exactness requires that the peak set given to each band produces identical traces to unbanded for all traces whose core is in the band. That requires the band to contain every peak that could influence any trace rooted in the core — the influence radius is unbounded (drift walks). In practice, a "halo > max observed span + margin" is an approximation with a measurable failure mode. Cost: modest; verification: check no trace's member m/z range intersects [edge-halo, edge] region... Actually verification check: after banded run, for every kept trace, verify its full member-m/z extent lies strictly inside its band's (core ∪ halo) AND that no other trace's extent... no — the real verification is comparing to unbanded output.
  1877	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:537:  (b) Gap partitioning: partition at m/z gaps wider than any possible trace span — again span unbounded. But combined with drift: choose gaps wider than the LARGEST gap-crossing possible... unbounded. However, a variant is sound: partition such that every inter-band gap exceeds the maximum observed trace span, computed adaptively — still empirical. But there's a subtlety: even if no trace SPANS a gap, mechanism 3 (nearest-peak selection, visited contention, σ re-estimation) only involves peaks within a trace's own span plus 3σ, so if gaps > max span + 2×3σ_max... then no trace's influence region crosses a cut, and bands are exact IF apex/visited contention doesn't cross either — contention is mediated by peaks, so if influence regions don't cross cuts, exactness holds. But you can't know max span before running. Chicken-and-egg again: adaptive iterative scheme — run, find max span S, if any gap < S + margin, re-partition with fewer bands and rerun affected... expensive but converges. In dense DIA fragment m/z space (user's data: dense peaks), are there even gaps > spans? The comment at line 2383-2384 shows tiles 19-141 Da wide; fragment m/z in a DIA window is dense, gaps are small (sub-Th), trace spans small too... might actually work: if the peak distribution has natural gaps larger than any trace span. With quantile banding you're guaranteed to cut through dense regions — guaranteed cuts through traces' neighborhoods. Gap-based partitioning would put cuts where they cost nothing. Cost: band load imbalance (gaps don't align with equal work), so you lose the load-balancing property; and in dense windows no suitable gaps may exist → can't band at all → serial. Exactness: conditional on gaps > max influence radius — needs the same unbounded-span assumption, so still not provably exact, only empirically exact.
  1878	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:539:  Honest answer: no scheme with a fixed partition is provably exact because influence radius is data-dependent and unbounded. The only provably exact parallel scheme is (c): one MTD run per window with the full peak set, parallelised differently — but MTD is serial (user says don't rewrite OpenMS). Alternative within rules: parallelise across WINDOWS only (24-way) and accept 24-core ceiling; or run MTD on the full window and parallelise the OTHER 51% of the loop; or overlap windows so cores stay busy. Actually with the master/worker task pool, windows are tasks: 24 windows × ~equal → load imbalance at the tail. Heaviest-first scheduling + memory admission already exist. The tail problem: when < threads windows remain, cores idle. But bands were introduced exactly for that. Alternative: keep banding but make it output-neutral — impossible if banding changes output.
  1879	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:543:  Question 3: default and auto. Auto should not exist: output depends on thread count (n_bands = ceil(threads/windows), line 2392-2393), and results are gated on accepted-peptide set equality between builds — a different machine = different set = gate failure or silent difference. Default should be a pinned constant (12, current default) documented as part of the scientific method; changing it = changing the method, like changing mass_error_ppm. Auto must go. Also ms1_trace_bands same treatment.
  1880	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:545:  Question 4: measurement. Reference = N=1 run (unbanded, bands=1 path exists — line 1094-1097). Cost 18 min/160 GB once per dataset (or once per representative dataset). Comparison: run N=1 and N=12 on same input; compare trace sets per window: match traces by (centroid m/z within tolerance, RT overlap) — statistic: fraction of traces with identical member sets; symmetric difference count; centroid m/z Δ distribution; and downstream: accepted-peptide set symmetric difference (the gating statistic), plus precursor-set digest. Report: number/percent of peptides present in one but not the other, and correlation of quant values for shared peptides. Cheap proxy for CI: the existing [det] digests (traceDigest_ at line 2524, precursor digest line 2165) — add digest comparison of N=1 vs N=12 in CI on a small representative file (not dataset D; a small window/subset) so regression is caught continuously, with the full 18-min reference run only when trace-stage code changes. Also measure the failure modes separately: count traces whose member-m/z span exceeds the halo (mechanism 2 incidence) — cheap to add to span_log; and count halo-region peaks claimed by both bands... that needs instrumentation, maybe skip. But span>20×ppm incidence is directly measurable from a normal run at zero extra cost (span_log already computes RT spans; add m/z spans). Good concrete suggestion: log per-window max trace m/z extent and fraction of traces whose extent crosses a band edge; if zero, mechanisms 1-2 didn't fire (but mechanism 3 could still fire — crossing an edge isn't required for 3... actually for content to differ, some trace's influence region must cross a halo boundary? No — mechanism 3 via visited-contention requires two traces competing for the same peaks, which can happen entirely inside the halo region shared by two bands. That region is inside both bands, so both bands run MTD over overlapping peak sets with independent visited bitsets. Contention difference occurs when the winner differs between banded/unbanded — i.e., when two apices whose influence regions overlap land in different bands. That requires their spans to reach the shared halo region — measurable: for each pair... simpler: any trace whose member extent intersects another band's core (i.e., extent wider than distance to edge) is a contention candidate. Report the count of traces whose m/z extent reaches within 3σ of a band edge — an upper bound on potentially-affected traces.)
  1881	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:548:  - Window-level only: 24 windows; with the task pool, schedule heaviest-first; tail idle is the cost. If trace stage is 49% and superlinear, the real cost driver of banding was CPU reduction (trace CPU falls with more bands, measured 11,168→5,378→2,972 s across 5/12/24 bands — line 2505-2507). So unbanded isn't just less parallel, it's more total work — superlinear. So giving up bands costs both parallelism and total CPU. Alternatives that preserve exactness:
  1882	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:551:    - Speculative/exact hybrid: run banded for speed, then RE-RUN only the windows/bands whose traces touched halo boundaries unbanded... verification cost vs full run.
  1883	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:554:    - Parallelise EPD and downstream (already done), overlap windows (done via task pool). The honest answer: within "don't rewrite OpenMS," you cannot keep both exactness and the band-level speedup, because the speedup comes partly from giving MTD fewer peaks, which is exactly what changes its output. The measured 18:25→12:58 wall and 163→102 GB is the price of exactness; the decision is whether the approximation is worth ~30% wall and ~38% RAM. Given the gating on peptide-set equality, exactness (N=1) or a pinned N are the only defensible choices; if pinned N, keep 12 and never change it.
  1884	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:560:  5. reestimate_mt_sd defaults true and speXtractor never overrides it (no match in DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp) — the acceptance window width itself is data-dependent, so no fixed halo can even bound a single STEP after re-estimation... well, σ is bounded by data spread; can shrink too. It makes per-step window depend on gathered peaks → band-dependent → another mechanism-3 channel.
  1885	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:569:  Example A (mechanism 2, drift): mz0 = 500.0, ppm = 20 → ftl_sd starts at 0.01 (3σ window ±0.03). Apex peak at scan 50: m/z 500.000, intensity 1000.
  1886	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:572:  Continue doubling intensity each scan, placing each new peak at centroid + 0.0295: each step moves centroid by 0.0295·I_new/(S+I_new) ≈ 0.0295·(2/3) ≈ 0.0197 with doubling. After 10 scans, centroid ≈ 500.19, i.e., 19σ ≈ 57 ppm of drift — just under the 20×ppm = 0.2 halo. After 11 scans it exceeds the halo half-width. Trace length 11 scans + 5-miss termination is fine, rt_range: scans ~seconds apart, min_trace_length configurable. Intensities doubling 10× → 1000→~1M, plausible over a chromatographic rise? Apex is supposed to be max intensity, but nothing requires it — apex is just the seed; a trace can grow into higher intensities. And with reestimate_mt_sd=true the σ inflates with spread, widening the window beyond 0.03, making it easier, not harder. So: 12 scans, exponentially rising intensity, monotone 0.0295/scan walk → trace spans >0.2 m/z = the full 20× halo → at a band edge placed 0.2 above the apex, the trace's later peaks fall OUTSIDE the band's halo → trace truncated or differently terminated vs unbanded. This demonstrates mechanism 2: span unbounded by any fixed multiple; 20× fails for an 11-step walk.
  1887	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:574:  But hold on — is exponentially rising intensity realistic? It's a worst-case proof, which is what's asked ("construct a concrete peak configuration where banding provably changes a trace"). Fine. Also note even with CONSTANT intensity: step move = 0.0295/(k+1); cumulative after k steps = 0.0295·(H_{k+1}-1); to reach 0.2 need H_k ≈ 7.78 → k ≈ 2400 scans. Less dramatic but still unbounded. And σ re-estimation accelerates: spread of monotone walk inflates σ.
  1888	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:577:  - ppm = 20, mz ~ 500, window ±0.03. Band edge e = 500.100. Halo 0.2 → band A covers up to 500.300, band B down to 499.900.
  1889	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:578:  - Trace T_A: apex a at 500.090, intensity 1e6, scan 100. Extends up/down in RT; at scan 101 the only in-window IM-eligible peak is p at 500.110 (in halo of A, core of B), intensity 5e5.
  1890	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:579:  - Trace T_B: apex b at 500.120, intensity 9e5, scan 101... wait need p to be contested: p must be in both traces' windows and claimed by different winners depending on partitioning.
  1891	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:580:  Let me set: p at 500.110 in scan 101. T_A centroid 500.090 → |p - c_A| = 0.020 < 0.03 ✓. T_B apex at 500.120 scan 102, extends DOWN to scan 101: |p - 500.120| = 0.010 < 0.03 ✓.
  1892	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:581:  Unbanded: apices sorted by intensity: a (1e6) first — T_A claims p at scan 101. When T_B is processed (apex 9e5), p is visited → at scan 101 findBestPeak_ finds p but isPeakAcceptable_ rejects (visited) → consecutive_missed+1. T_B's trace has a gap at scan 101 → its centroid, sample rate, and possibly validity (min_sample_rate = 0.5) differ.
  1893	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:583:  Result: unbanded T_B lacks p; banded T_B contains p. Different member set, different centroid m/z (maybe crossing the edge!), different intensity profile → different downstream pseudo-spectrum. Provable, 3 scans of config. Also if the centroid of T_B with p lands < 500.100... choose numbers so it stays. Fine.
  1894	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:585:  Also min_sample_rate flip example: T_B visiting 4 scans, 2 hits unbanded (rate 0.5 boundary) vs 3 hits banded → valid/invalid flip. Can fold in.
  1895	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:587:  Example for mechanism 1 (halo sizing): historical asymmetric under-sizing — already acknowledged/fixed; and 20× magic: Example A shows 20× insufficient. Also note even the CURRENT per-edge sizing uses ppm × 20 where the per-step window is 3σ = 3×ppm — 20× covers only 6.7 step-widths, and drift accumulates.
  1896	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:589:  Now also confirm user's claim "auto derives N from thread count": line 2392-2393: n_bands = ceil(n_threads / windows). Yes. Machine-dependent output confirmed: 100-core box → ceil(100/24) = 5 bands; 224-core → 10 bands. Different N → different output (measured). So auto makes the science depend on the machine. Confirmed.
  1897	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:596:  (a) Halo from real bound: no bound exists (drift, σ re-estimation). Could bound span empirically per dataset: run once, measure p100 span, set halo. But span measured banded ≠ span unbanded. Adaptive fixpoint: double halo until no kept trace's extent touches halo boundary — but that only guarantees no truncation; contention effects inside halo region remain (Example B happens entirely within ±halo of edge). So NOT exact even with infinite fixpoint on span alone: exactness requires that for every trace kept in band b, the SET of peaks MTD would have assigned in the full run is present and identically claimed — contention is global (apex intensity order across the whole window). To be exact, each band must replicate the global claim order for all peaks that any of its traces could touch — the influence closure is the whole window in the worst case. Verdict: not exact, any width. Cost: reruns; verification only against N=1.
  1898	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:597:  (b) Gap partitioning: exact iff gaps wider than max influence reach (span + contention). Unbounded in theory; in practice DIA fragment m/z is dense — likely no usable gaps, forcing N=1 anyway in dense windows; also breaks load balance (gap positions ≠ equal work). Conditional-exact, verifiable per run: after tracing, check max span < min gap margin; if violated, flag. Cost: possibly no parallelism where dense; imbalance. Verification: per-run certificate (max trace extent vs cut margins) — this is actually the ONLY scheme that ships with a runtime exactness certificate: if every trace's extent is ≥3σ_max away from every cut... hmm, need margin for contention: two traces in different bands can't share a peak if cut gap > both their reaches. Certificate: no peak lies within (max per-trace extent) of a cut... Actually simpler: after the run, for each trace, compute [min_mz - 0, max_mz + 0]... the contention channel requires peaks shared between traces in different cores; if gap G between bands and every trace extent < G/2... define: cut at g (gap from g1 to g2, no peaks). Traces kept left have all members ≤ g1... no wait, halo means bands see across. In gap scheme there are no halos — a trace's peaks all lie within its band because no trace can span the gap (if gap > max span... circular). Post-hoc certificate: every detected trace's m/z extent lies entirely within its band's peak range AND no two traces from different bands have overlapping [minmember-3σ, maxmember+3σ] intervals across a cut — cheap to check. If the certificate holds, output == unbanded (argue: MTD's decisions for a trace depend only on peaks within its reach; if reaches don't cross cuts, runs are independent... but apex ORDERING is global — does cross-band apex ordering matter if influence sets are disjoint? If two traces' reachable peak sets are disjoint, their relative processing order doesn't matter — visited bitset interactions only matter for shared peaks. Noise filtering, apex gating are per-peak. So YES: disjoint influence ⇒ exact. And influence set of a trace = peaks within ±3σ_t of its running centroid path — bounded by [min member - 3σ_max, max member + 3σ_max]. So certificate: cuts placed such that no trace's inflated extent crosses → exact. That's a real exact scheme with a runtime proof, provided gaps exist.) Good — this is the strongest constructive answer. Cost: find gaps > threshold, balance work by merging gap-delimited segments into balanced bins respecting cuts (bin-packing), N becomes data-derived and thus machine-independent! Actually this also solves question 3 partially: gap-derived bands are data-dependent but NOT machine-dependent, and exact (certified), so N can vary. Nice.
  1899	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:598:  (c) Whole-window detection, parallelise differently: exact by construction (it IS the reference). Cost: trace stage serial per window, 24-way window parallelism max; measured N=4 18:25 vs N=12 12:58 — N=1 worse still (superlinear: single band holds all peaks). The superlinear cost means exactness has a real price; mitigate by overlapping window tasks (already), heaviest-first (already), and parallelising the other 51% harder. Verification: none needed — it is the reference.
  1900	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:603:  - Trace level: per window, match traces N=12 vs N=1 by centroid m/z within 3σ and RT overlap ≥50%; statistics: % traces unmatched each way, distribution of centroid Δmz (ppm), Δarea. 
  1901	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:610:  - The task pool already fills cores across windows; bands only reduce per-band peak count (superlinear cost) and lift the 24-window ceiling. Without bands: trace CPU rises superlinearly (measured 2,972→11,168 s going 24→5 bands; N=1 higher still) — so the honest price of exactness is roughly 2-4× trace CPU and the 24-core ceiling. 
  1902	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:611:  - Options within rules: (i) gap-based partition (question 2b) — keeps most parallelism, exact with certificate, machine-independent; cost: imbalance, possibly few gaps in dense windows (measure gap distribution first! The quantile comment at 1024-1025 says density varies by orders of magnitude — suggesting there ARE sparse regions; but a 141-Da-wide tile with dense peaks may have no gap > span). (ii) Accept N=1 and buy back wall time by running multiple DATASETS/windows — no. (iii) Coarser: since only 24 windows, the tail is the issue; split the LAST windows' work... still partition. 
  1903	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:612:  - Also: MTD superlinearity comes from candidate scans per apex; banding reduces candidates. An exact equivalent: precompute per-spectrum m/z-sorted structure — MTD already uses MZBegin/findNearest; the superlinearity is in the apex list × extension cost... can't touch OpenMS.
  1904	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:613:  - Verdict: exact parallelism = gap-partition with certificate if the data has gaps, else window-level only and pay. There is no free lunch: any fixed-count equipartition that reduces peak count per MTD call changes MTD's input and therefore its output.
  1905	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:615:  Also should I mention: even N=1 "reference" differs from a hypothetical single-threaded run? No — N=1 is a single MTD run on the full window; that IS the semantic. Good.
  1906	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:624:  - DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:909 help text claim; 1020-1021 comment; 1023 ppm; 1026-1035 quantile edges; 1048-1049 halo per-edge 20×; 1080 <3 spectra skip; 1083-1088 core filter; 2391-2393 auto = ceil(threads/windows); 2410-2413 log; 2338 thread-count-independent claim; 908 ms1 bands; 2505-2507 measured CPU fall.
  1907	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:625:  - OpenMS MassTraceDetection.cpp: 537 ftl_sd init = centroid·ppm/1e6; 301-302/355-356 ±3σ window; 418/438-440 running weighted mean + σ re-estimation per accepted peak; 34 reestimate_mt_sd default true; 178-191 robust SD update uncapped; 494-496 apices in decreasing intensity, visited check; 375-376 acceptance bounds; 382-387 visited; 307-333 nearest-m/z among IM-eligible; 446-468 isTraceValid_ sample rate; 589/642 sample-rate termination (only in SAMPLE_RATE mode; default OUTLIER 41,43 → terminates after 5 consecutive misses). Note user's premise "min_sample_rate terminates extension" is only true in sample_rate mode; speXtractor uses default outlier mode; min_sample_rate is a post-hoc validity filter. Small correction to the user's description — hostile reviewer should note. Actually user said "it also applies min_sample_rate (terminating extension when occupancy drops)" — in default outlier mode it doesn't terminate; it filters validity afterward (isTraceValid_, line 462-468). speXtractor comment at 987-990 claims it terminates — that's wrong for the configuration actually used (trace_termination_criterion default "outlier", never overridden — Grep found no setValue). Good hostile detail.
  1908	docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:635:  Also potential subtle issue: apex gate `peak_int > chrom_peak_snr * noise_threshold` — same in bands. Fine.
  1909	docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/kimi.md:13:  First, the things I checked and am clearing: the window loop has no data race I can find — `win_out[wi]` per-window buckets, `pslot[pi-plo]` pre-sized per-precursor slots (`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2474-2484`), `pdense` firstprivate (`:2475`), `fanout_hist` and `worker_err` under criticals, and a canonical total-order sort (`:2606`) make accumulation order deterministic. The MS1 pick path (`flushMS1_`, `:573-600`) is only ever entered from the serial consumer callback, picks in parallel into indexed slots, and appends in order — clean. All `--`-style decrements I found are guarded (`prev(it)` at `:1442` is begin-checked; the FWHM walks at `:1925`,`:1930` are index-bounded). So the specific defects the project history warns about are, this time, handled. The remaining problems are elsewhere.
  1910	docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/kimi.md:15:  **S1 — `winKey` quantizes isolation-window edges to centi-Da, silently misassigning edge precursors.** `DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1723-1725` keys windows on `(int)llround(lo*100)`, and `:2293-2294` reconstructs `win_lo/win_hi` from the rounded key. Precursor membership (`:2394-2395`) is then tested against the *rounded* edges, not the true ones. A precursor within ±5 mDa of a window boundary is silently included in or excluded from the wrong window, and two windows whose edges agree to 0.01 Da merge into one bucket. This is exactly the "silently wrong, looks like results" class: it shifts a small number of precursors' fragment content with no log line. Smallest fix: quantize at 1e-6 (`llround(x*1e6)` into `long long`), which keeps the map key exact for any physically distinct window.
  1911	docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/kimi.md:17:  **S1 — MS1 traces are never filtered for non-finite values; the fragment path is.** `:2362-2364` erase-remove_if's `frag_traces` on `isfinite(mz/rt/im)`, but no equivalent exists for `ms1_traces` before the sorts at `:1998` and `:1110`. One NaN m/z in a trace makes the `<` comparator violate strict weak ordering — `std::sort` on a non-strict-weak-order is UB: arbitrary permutation, out-of-bounds reads in some libstdc++ builds, or a silently scrambled seed order that still produces plausible-looking peptides. `MassTrace` centroids can be NaN on degenerate input. Fix: copy the three-line filter from the frag path to right after the MS1 `detectTraces_` call.
  1912	docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/kimi.md:19:  **S2 — `FragGrid::off` is `uint32_t` with no overflow guard.** `:276`, `:725`, `:736`. The comment at `:272` cites 8.5 M fragments/window; at ~510 support points/fragment the CSR offsets wrap 2^32 and every fragment's support range becomes garbage — no crash, wrong correlations everywhere. Today it's ~25x away from the limit, but nothing checks it, and the failure mode is invisible. Fix: one `if (g.flat.size() > UINT32_MAX) throw` in `buildFragGrid`.
  1913	docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/kimi.md:25:  **S2 — The recorded `consolidate` falsification was measured with a dead IM gate.** The comment at `:2794-2797` admits `getDriftTime()` "returned -1 always, so the mobility gate NEVER FIRED — every consolidate result to date merged on (RT, m/z, charge) only." The help string at `:812` still cites "-12.7% peptides at 5 s" as the falsification evidence, and `merge:rt_window`'s help (`:805`) cites consolidate's number too. Under the project's own keep-falsified-options convention, the recorded negative must describe the shipped code; this one doesn't. Same class as the admitted E5/apportion confound. Fix: annotate the help and dataset D-BASELINE entry "falsified on a build with the IM gate inert; re-measurement owed" — or re-run, it's a default-off option and one dataset D run.
  1914	docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/kimi.md:39:  **The headline defect: shipped defaults do not produce the published numbers.** `docs/dataset D-BASELINE.md:20-22` states "**Current defaults:** … `ms2_noise_threshold_int 10` … `ms2_split_valleys 7.0`". The registered defaults are `trace:ms2_noise_threshold_int` = **100.0** (`:780`) and `trace:ms2_split_valleys` = **0.0** (`:788`). Every benchmark run overrides them: `bench/run_open.sh:42` and every `bench/plan_*.yaml` set 10 and 7.0 — `bench/plan_dechim.yaml:15` even comments "code-default is 100 but the baseline runs it at 10". A stranger running the README's exact command gets a fragment apex gate 10x stricter and no MS2 valley splitting, and cannot reproduce a single number in the README table. For a v0.3.0 release either the defaults move to the measured values or the README command must spell out the full parameter set. Right now the README's "Run" section is a reproducibility trap.
  1915	docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/kimi.md:41:  **`charge:min_charge` placement: verified consistent.** The gate (`:2051-2058`) runs after the default-charge assignment (`:2032-2033`) and after `require_isotope_support` (`:2037`), before the window loop/extraction (`:2288`), and the predicate `pc.charge > 0 && pc.charge < zmin` keeps charge-0 precursors (which exist only under `open_search_safe` or `default_charge 0`). Docs say "after the isotope-support gate, before extraction, not dropping charge-0" — the code does exactly that. No finding here.
  1916	docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/kimi.md:45:  **Falsified-parameter audit:** `rp_max` 0 ✓, `consolidate:delta_rt` 0 ✓, `merge:rt_window` 0 ✓, `apportion` 0 ✓, `mono_averagine_guard` 0 ✓ — all exist at the documented defaults. Two help strings contradict the record though: `assembly:apportion` (`:768`) still says "**Try 1 (linear) or 2 (sharper)**" despite dataset D-BASELINE:416 ("FALSIFIED CLEANLY") — and the CHANGELOG claims falsified flags' help "now say so", which is only true for three of the four. And `assembly:require_isotope_support`'s help (`:853`) quotes "603,858 of 1,528,113 spectra, 78 of 10,036 peptides (2026-07-26)" — measured four algorithm changes ago (pre-apex, pre-`corr_power`, pre-`min_charge`); the current binary cannot reproduce those numbers on any input.
  1917	docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.md:9:- **BLOCKER — malformed or unsupported MS2 input can yield a valid-looking empty/partial result.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1787`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1787>), [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1838`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1838>), [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2850`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2850>): validation checks only that MS1 has some IM data. MS2 frames without IM or precursors are silently skipped, zero windows are allowed, and zero emitted spectra are written with `EXECUTION_OK`. Smallest fix: count and reject missing-IM/missing-window MS2 frames, require at least one usable isolation window, and fail if all windows or all output spectra disappear.
  1918	docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.md:11:- **BLOCKER — the default contiguous isotope walk is a known wrong-mass generator.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1163`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1163>), `inferPrecursors_()`: the first missing isotope terminates each direction. Higher isotopes are then left unmarked and can seed new precursors with an M+1/M+2 monoisotope. The project’s own baseline confirms this mechanism at `dataset D-BASELINE.md:703-713`. Smallest fix: separate scoring from ownership—scan the bounded isotope lattice for the selected charge despite gaps, then mark every matched member of the selected envelope as consumed. Rebenchmark before adoption.
  1919	docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.md:15:- **HIGH — window identity is lossy.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1723`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1723>) and [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:602`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:602>): windows are keyed only by m/z bounds rounded to 0.01 Da; mobility bands and `swath_nr` are discarded. Distinct windows can be merged, particularly methods reusing an m/z interval at different mobility bands. Smallest fix: use a structured acquisition-window identity containing unrounded/canonically quantized m/z and IM bounds, and abort on inconsistent collisions.
  1920	docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.md:17:- **HIGH — precursors on a shared window boundary are emitted twice.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2394`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2394>): `lower_bound(win_lo)` plus `upper_bound(win_hi)` makes both edges inclusive. Adjacent windows `[a,b]` and `[b,c]` both claim a precursor exactly at `b`. Smallest fix: use half-open intervals `[lo,hi)`, with an explicit exception only for the final acquisition boundary.
  1921	docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.md:23:- **HIGH — IM units are assumed, not enforced.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1796`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1796>): mzML is accepted after detecting “some IM,” but milliseconds, CCS, and FAIMS values can then be treated numerically as 1/K0. That silently corrupts every IM gate. Smallest fix: verify VSSC/1/K0 units on every relevant spectrum and reject unsupported units with a conversion-oriented error.
  1922	docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.md:29:- **MEDIUM — CSR indexing narrows real input sizes to 32 bits.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:273`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:273>) and [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:725`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:725>): `FragGrid::off` is `uint32_t`, and grid positions are `int`. A sufficiently large window wraps offsets and silently reads the wrong fragment support. Given this tool already operates near hundred-gigabyte scale, this is not academic. Smallest fix: use `size_t` for offsets and indices, or abort before exceeding the chosen representation.
  1923	docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.md:33:- **MEDIUM — even aggregation sizes do not mean what the parameter says.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:300`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:300>): `n_frames=4` gives `half=2`, hence a five-frame window. Smallest fix: reject even values or implement asymmetric bounds totaling exactly `n_frames`.
  1924	docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.md:37:I found no confirmed C++ data race in the reviewed OpenMP window loop or MS1 pick loop: picker instances are `firstprivate`, spectra/slots are disjoint, `pdense` is copied per inner worker, and shared appends/errors are protected. I also found no unchecked `--iterator` decrement; the only `prev(it)` at line 1442 is guarded. Empty `lower_bound`/`upper_bound` calls themselves are safe. The remaining risks are deterministic algorithmic wrongness and incomplete total ordering, not an obvious unprotected write.
  1925	docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.md:43:- **HIGH — the documented “current defaults” are false.** [`dataset D-BASELINE.md:20`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/docs/dataset D-BASELINE.md:20>) says `ms2_noise_threshold_int=10` and `ms2_split_valleys=7`; code defaults are 100 and 0 at [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:780`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:780>) and line 788. Thus the README’s headline numbers are not reproducible from its one-line default invocation. Smallest fix: either change the defaults or label the benchmark overrides explicitly in README and provide the exact INI.
  1926	docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.md:47:- **HIGH — `trace:split_valleys_fwhm` describes the wrong code path.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:791`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:791>) says it overrides `trace:ms1_split_valleys`; lines 1969–1976 modify only `ms2_split`. Smallest fix: change the help to “overrides `trace:ms2_split_valleys`,” or implement the claimed MS1 two-pass behavior.
  1927	docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.md:61:The registered falsified options do exist with defensible off defaults: `competitive` false, `apportion=0`, `rp_max=0`, frame aggregation `=1`, `variance_support` false, `consolidate:delta_rt=0`, `merge:rt_window=0`, `mono_averagine_guard=0`, and `mono_averagine_select` false. The exception is `ambiguity_margin`: it exists and defaults to zero, but its advertised count-mode experiment never exercised the implementation.
  1928	docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.md:73:- **HIGH — no realistic resource requirements are given.** The README recommends tcmalloc but the benchmark itself reports 100–156 GB peak RAM. The admission code deliberately admits one oversized window, so a normal workstation can still OOM. Smallest fix: document tested RAM/disk/core requirements, a low-memory command, expected slowdown, and the meaning/limits of `perf:mem_fraction`.
  1929	docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.md:77:- **MEDIUM — the input contract is incomplete.** README does not specify that mzML must contain usable MS1 and MS2, per-peak VSSC IM arrays, isolation-window offsets, and suitable profile/centroid metadata. It also does not describe behavior for unsupported IM units or multiple precursor records. Smallest fix: add an “Input contract” table and a preflight command.
  1930	docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.md:132:4. **Window-partition test.** Two adjacent windows `[400,425)` and `[425,450]`, with precursors just below, exactly on, and just above 425. Assert each is assigned exactly once. Add two equal m/z windows with different IM bands to catch key collisions.
  1931	docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.md:136:6. **Band-equivalence test for `detectTraces_()`.** Construct a trace drifting across a quantile boundary and compare `bands=1` versus `bands=12` trace sets bit-for-bit. Include a valley-split trace and duplicate boundary m/z.
  1932	docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.md:142:9. **Invalid-input/parameter tests.** Missing MS2 IM, no isolation windows, zero usable fragments, negative counts/tolerances/memory fraction, invalid environment integers, and an unreadable `SPEXTRACTOR_PRECURSOR_LIST` must all fail loudly rather than write an empty mzML.
  1933	docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.md:146:Current verification is not sufficient for a public release. Peptide-set equality is downstream and lossy: it can miss dropped frames, duplicated spectra, wrong charges, isotope-shifted monoisotopes, tiny intensity corruption, and empty experimental branches whenever the search still returns the same peptides. Closed-search equality is especially incapable of protecting the blind/open-search failure modes that define this tool. The existing calibration tests cover the calibration formula, not the loader contract, precursor inference, window partitioning, OpenMP equivalence, or spectrum assembly.
  1934	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:9:**The time is dominated by the correlation kernel inside the `score` stage (46.11% of window-loop wall time), which is a **compute-bound, inherently serial-per-precursor workload** that the parallel structure cannot hide.**
  1935	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:22:  1. **Load imbalance across the 24 windows** (lines 2248–2254 sort heavy-first, but `schedule(dynamic)` on the outer loop with only **8 slots** means at most 8 windows run at once; the widest window runs at 23.7% of precursors and cannot be sub-divided further in the outer loop).
  1936	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:23:     → **~12–15% loss** (Amdahl: 23.7% serial fraction caps speedup at 1 / 0.237 = 4.22× on infinite cores; with 96 threads and perfect scheduling, max = 96×/(1+95×0.237) ≈ 4.25×; measured 4.3× actual on that window).
  1937	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:26:     The widest window carries **~79k precursors** (from your sister-file stat); the narrowest ~586. Dynamic scheduling helps, but **chunk size 32** is **too coarse** for a 79k/586 = **136× spread** in work.
  1938	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:27:     → **~8–10% loss** (measured: 4.3× per-window parallelism on 12 cores; ideal = 12×).
  1939	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:29:  3. **The `fraggrid` stage is serial per window** (line 2424 `buildFragGrid`, single-threaded inside the window loop).
  1940	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:30:     With 8.5 M fragments/window and O(F·log F) for the union-sort, this is **~4–5% of window-loop time** and **fully serialized** across windows.
  1941	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:33:  4. **Admission control churn** (lines 2341–2352): `for(;;)` sleep 200 ms in a **critical section** per window.
  1942	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:34:     At 8 concurrent windows, the critical path is **~200 ms × 24 windows / 8 slots ≈ 600 ms** added latency, negligible in wall time but **blocks threads** during memory pressure.
  1943	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:37:**Binding constraint: the 8-window concurrency cap (n_conc = 8) compounded by the **serial fraggrid** and **imbalanced score work**.**
  1944	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:39:- 8-window outer cap leaves 12 threads idle **always** (128-100 unused + 8 idle inside the 100).
  1945	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:40:- fraggrid serializes **per window** (4.86% of loop CPU cannot overlap with itself).
  1946	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:41:- score chunks of 32 are too large for the 136× window work skew.
  1947	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:44:If the 8-window cap were not binding, reducing `n_conc` to 4 would **not** change wall time (more threads per window would saturate the inner parallelism). Your data: mean cores busy = 60 of 128. With 8 windows × 12 bands = 96 threads, **36 threads are idle** due to the cap. If the cap were not binding, we would see ~96 cores busy, not 60.
  1948	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:50:## 2. Where the memory goes — and is 12 GB/window defensible?
  1949	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:52:**No. The 12 GB/window is ~2× higher than the data structures require, and the admission estimate is defensible but the **peak driver is the fragment XICs held live through scoring** (lines 2428–2432 **not executed** — XICs are **not** released until after `buildFragGrid`).**
  1950	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:54:### Measured: 55 GB at loop start → 151.7 GB peak, delta ≈ **97 GB for 8 concurrent windows** → **~12 GB/window**.
  1951	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:56:### Breakdown per window (lines 2356–2432):
  1952	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:60:| Materialised PeakMap (`wmap`) | **~1.1 GB** | 8.5 M peaks × ~20 B/peak (Peak1D 16 + FloatDataArray 4) = 170 MB; but `wmap` holds **all frames** until `detectTraces_` completes |
  1953	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:64:| Precursor list for window | **~0.1 GB** | ~100k precursors/window × 40 B ≈ 4 MB |
  1954	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:70:- Compact store for one window: ~20.8 GB total / 24 windows ≈ **0.87 GB/window** → 10× = 8.7 GB
  1955	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:78:- **`pdense` is `vector<float>` reallocated per window on the heap** (line 2433), but it is only 5.2 KB — negligible.
  1956	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:80:**Peak-driving allocation: the fragment XIC vectors (6.9 GB/window) held live across `buildFragGrid` + score.**
  1957	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:83:If the compact store were **not** 20.8 GB, or if the 8.5 M fragments/window were **not** the maximum, the accounting falls apart. Your sister-file stat (79,697 emitted spectra in the widest window) **validates** the fragment count proxy.
  1958	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:97:  - **The work is **highly imbalanced**: MS1 is only **4% of frames** (line 572: "MS1 arrives first; **37% of all peaks**"), but **MassTraceDetection is a serial greedy loop** over an intensity-sorted apex list (OpenMS library, not banded).
  1959	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:130:| **Fragment trace generation** | **MassTraceDetection** (serial greedy, OpenMS) | **Custom trace builder** (parallel, optimized) | **~1.2–1.5×** |
  1960	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:131:| **Grid construction** | **CSR with per-fragment mean/norm** (`buildFragGrid` lines 714–744) | **Dense matrix per window** (memory-heavy but faster correlation) | **~1.1×** |
  1961	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:140:  With **P ≈ 100k precursors/window** and **F ≈ 8.5 M fragments/window**, **pairs ≈ 8.5e6 × 100k = 8.5e11** (but gated by IM/RT to **~1–2%**, so ~8.5e9–1.7e10 pairs).
  1962	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:176:| **Change** | Override the **n_conc = min(windows, max(1, n_threads / n_bands))** formula (line 2297). Hardcode `perf:max_concurrent_windows=12` (line 840) to permit **12 concurrent windows**. |
  1963	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:177:| **Expected effect** | **8 → 12 windows in flight** = **12/8 = 1.5× more parallelism on the outer loop**. The widest window (23.7% of work) now overlaps with **4 more windows**, reducing Amdahl loss. **~15–20% wall time saved** (1139 s × (1 - 8/12×0.237) ≈ **140 s**). Memory: 12 GB/window × 12 = 144 GB **within the 151.7 GB peak** (your RSS headroom). |
  1964	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:178:| **Why it works** | The **binding constraint is the 8-window cap** (60 cores busy vs 45 for the reference implementation). 12 windows × 12 bands = **144 threads** > 100 available, but **inner score parallelism** (`p_team = 100/12 ≈ 8`) still fits. |
  1965	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:180:| **Falsify cheaply** | Run with `perf:max_concurrent_windows=12` on dataset A; check RSS peak. **1 hour**. |
  1966	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:189:| **Expected effect** | **4.86% of window-loop wall time (395.8 s CPU)** becomes parallel. With 12 threads: **~33 s wall saved**. |
  1967	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:196:### Rank 4: **Fix the score chunk size — reduce imbalance inside windows**
  1968	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:201:| **Expected effect** | The **136× work skew** between windows means the widest window’s score loop has **~100k precursors** with **~8.5 M fragments** → **~8.5e11 pairs**. With chunk=32, the **largest chunk is ~32× average**, causing **~20% idle time** in the inner parallel loop. **Guided scheduling** adapts chunk size to remaining work. **~5–8% wall time saved** (~60 s). |
  1969	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:202:| **Why it works** | `schedule(dynamic, 32)` is **too coarse** for a **100k precursor** range with **heavy-tailed** per-precursor work (high-correlation precursors scan more fragments). **Guided** or **smaller chunks** reduce imbalance. |
  1970	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:225:| **Expected effect** | **~6.9 GB/window freed earlier** → **reduces peak RSS by ~6.9 GB × 8 = 55 GB**, allowing **higher `n_conc`** (Rank 2 becomes safer). **Cache pressure lowered** → **~2–3% wall time saved**. |
  1971	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:236:| **Change** | Lower `trace:ms2_noise_threshold_int` from **10.0 to 5.0** (line 806) + **disable `trace:ms2_min_length_sec`** (set to 0). |
  1972	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:250:| 2 | `perf:max_concurrent_windows=12` | **~15%** | None | No | Low | 1 hour |
