ds of the pool... The inner parallel regions create teams of 12 — which threads? Nested parallelism: each of the 8 outer threads creates its own team of 12 → 8×12=96. The OpenMP runtime keeps a global thread pool; threads not in a team wait. With GNU OMP_WAIT_POLICY unset, waiting threads SPIN (GOMP defaults to spinning with implicit wait policy... GOMP_SPINCOUNT default is 300,000 when OMP_WAIT_POLICY not set — actually if unset, GOMP uses spin count 300000 for active? The default in libgomp: OMP_WAIT_POLICY unset → "spin for a while then sleep" with GOMP_SPINCOUNT default 300000 (on non-SPINLOCK...). Hmm, historically libgomp default is to spin ~300k iterations (≈ ms-scale), then futex sleep. That's small per wait but with heavily imbalanced bands, threads sit at barriers for seconds — they'd sleep after ms of spinning. So spin waste is bounded-ish but could still add CPU. The measured gap: reserved slot-time 97,674 - CPU 77,333 = 20,341 s idle. If idle threads slept, they don't burn CPU — consistent with CPU < reserved. But could spinning add beyond? CPU 77,333 vs busy... we can't tell how much of the 77,333 is useful vs spin. The phase table CPU/wall for whole run: 22.9h/22.6min... 82,440 s CPU / 1,359 s wall = 60.7 mean cores busy — matches user's "60 of 128".
