  2078	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:127:  Also is there oversubscription? n_conc×n_bands = 96 ≤ 100. But LOAD ran at 81.7x with 100 threads. MS1_TRACE only 5.0x despite 12 bands (perf:ms1_trace_bands default 12). Why only 5x? Look at MS1 detectTraces_ call: bands=ms1_bands=12. MS1 map: 1,343 frames × ~690k peaks... The band partition at :969-997 is SERIAL (single thread) over the whole MS1 map — 1,343 spectra × 12 bands with binary-search slicing; that builds 12 sub-PeakMaps copying all MS1 peaks (~37% of 2.19e9 peaks = 8.1e8 peaks... wait, that's peaks including all frames; MS1 peaks ~ 0.37×2.19e9 = 8.1e8? That seems huge. "1,343 frames x ~690k raw peaks" — raw peaks; after picking fewer. Still, the serial partition copies every peak into band sub-maps (Peak1D 16B + float IM 4B + MSSpectrum overhead). That serial copy could be much of the 83 s wall. Then band tracing is 12-way parallel but quantile bands balance peaks not work; then EPD split at :1047 with num_threads(bands)=12, then toTrace serial, spans instrumentation serial. MS1_TRACE 83 s wall / 411 s CPU = 5.0x. Serial sections: partition copy + MassTrace param setup + merging + sort at :2048 (that's outside the phase timer? The MS1_TRACE phase timer ends right after detectTraces_ returns :1939 — includes partition, band trace, EPD, toTrace). So 5x = parallel sections partially cover serial partition. Worth fixing? MS1_TRACE is 6.1% wall; perfect 12x → saves ~76×(1-5/12)... wall 83 → ideal ~ 83×5/12 ≈ 34.6 s if same CPU perfectly parallel... CPU is only 411 s; at 12x it'd be 34 s wall; saves ~48 s = 3.6% of total wall. Cheap-ish fix but small gain. The partition itself could be parallelised or avoided (route peaks to bands during load — but load is streaming and doesn't know quantiles yet; could buffer). Given 6.1% share and risk, low priority but easy.
  2079	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:131:  Question 2: memory. RSS 55 GB at loop start → peak 151.7 GB, +97 GB across ≤8 windows ≈ 12 GB/window. Where does 12 GB/window go? Data structures per window:
  2080	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:132:  - materialized wmap: window compact bytes ×~2 (10 B/peak compact → 20 B/peak Peak1D+IM float + MSSpectrum overhead ~ hundreds of bytes × frames). Avg window compact = 20.8GB/24 ≈ 0.87 GB → wmap ≈ 1.8-2.5 GB. Big window: compact maybe 3-4 GB → wmap 7-9 GB.
  2081	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:133:  - Band partition sub[12]: COPIES all peaks + halo duplicates (:969-997) → another ~20 B/peak × (1+halo). So during trace, wmap is cleared at :998 AFTER partition — peak during trace = sub copies ≈ 2× compact-ish + MassTraceDetection internals.
  2082	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:134:  - MassTraceDetection itself: MassTrace struct 168 B header/trace + peaks. 8.5M traces × (168 + support × ~32 B) — if avg support 20-50 points... MassTrace stores Peak2D-ish (32 B?). 8.5M × (168 + 30×32) ≈ 9.6 GB. That's huge! Then EPD split copies chunks (move). Then Trace structs: xic vector<pair<double,double>> = 16 B/point + 24 B header → 8.5M × (24 + 30×16 + other scalars ~ 64) ≈ 4.8 GB. Then FragGrid: off 8B×8.5M = 68 MB + flat 8 B × total support. The comment says ~510 support points?! "8.5 M fragments x ~510 support points" — 8.5e6 × 510 × 8 B = 34.7 GB for ONE window's FragGrid?! That can't be right as typical; the comment says within 2× of 32-bit wrap: 2^32 = 4.29e9 entries; 8.5M × 510 = 4.34e9 — yes! So the biggest window's FragGrid.flat is ~4.3e9 × 8 B = 34.7 GB. Plus mean/invnorm 16 B × 8.5M = 136 MB. Plus g.rt grid doubles.
  2083	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:136:  So per-window memory during score phase: FragGrid up to ~35 GB?? But measured ~12 GB/window average. The widest window might indeed peak much higher — peak RSS 151.7 could be driven by the widest window's FragGrid overlapping others. The 32-bit wrap comment says off is uint64 BUT at :731 `g.off[i] = (uint32_t)g.flat.size();` — cast to uint32_t! And off is vector<uint64_t>. The comment claims 64-bit on purpose but the code casts to uint32_t — a real bug if flat exceeds 4.29e9 entries! Wait — read again: line 731: `g.off[i] = (uint32_t)g.flat.size();` — yes, explicit (uint32_t) cast stored into uint64_t vector. The comment at :279-282 says "64-bit on purpose: 8.5 M fragments x ~510 support points is within 2x of the 32-bit wrap, and an overflow here silently corrupts every correlation." But the cast truncates! That's a genuine latent bug: if g.flat.size() ever exceeds 2^32, offsets wrap and correlations read garbage silently. The code's own comment says the data is WITHIN 2X of the wrap. This is a great hostile-review catch. The fix: drop the cast (trivial). And the FragGrid itself is the peak-driving allocation: flat at 8 B/entry.
  2084	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:138:  Memory accounting per window (~12 GB avg):
  2085	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:141:  - MassTraceDetection output + EPD: trace structs.
  2086	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:144:  - pdense per score thread: float × G per thread (p_team=12, firstprivate) — G ~ 1300×... grid size G: union of fragment RTs ~ up to frames count (each window sampled once/cycle; ~1398 cycles?) So G ≈ number of frames in window ≈ 33,553/24 ≈ 1,400. pdense = 5.6 KB × 12 — negligible.
  2087	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:147:  The admission estimate 10× compact + 256 MB: for the big window: compact 20.8 GB total... if the widest window holds ~23.7% of precursors and similar share of peaks → ~5 GB compact → need = 50 GB + 0.25. Hmm, if budget = 0.75×(MemAvailable+RSS)... The measured 12 GB/window average is suspiciously close to need-average (10×0.87=8.7+0.26≈9 GB). Actual usage 12 GB > estimate 9 GB → the estimate UNDER-estimates the big windows? If the big window actually needs 3×10=30+ GB but uses ~35 GB (FragGrid!), the 10× multiplier under-covers exactly at the peak-driving structure (FragGrid ~7× compact for trace-dense windows). The estimate is defensible for the trace phase (wmap+sub ≈ 3× + traces) but NOT for the score phase (FragGrid alone can reach ~7× compact). Evidence: peak occurs in loop. Also inflight accounting: `need` held constant through whole window; actual footprint varies 3× over phases.
  2088	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:149:  What's the peak driver: 8 concurrent windows × (trace-phase transient wmap+sub OR score-phase FragGrid). If big windows run concurrently, FragGrids dominate. Also note: FragGrid flat holds intensities for ALL fragment traces, while the fragment traces' xic vectors are freed at :2432 — good.
  2089	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:151:  Also: 20,849 MB compact store persists through the loop? materializeWindow CONSUMES frames (swap-clear at :491-495) — freed as consumed. Good. So base 55 GB at loop start = compact 20.9 + ms1_map PeakMap (1,343 × 690k... wait that's raw; picked MS1 ~ 8.1e8 peaks? no. Hmm, 55 GB: compact 20.9 + ms1_map + ms1_traces (6.4M × (24+16×support+64)) ≈ 6.4M×~500 ≈ 3.2 GB + precursors + MS1 map maybe 15-20 GB (MS1 picked peaks). Anyway base is measured, no need to fully explain.
  2090	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:153:  Is something copied that need not be? YES: the band partition at :969-997 copies every peak of the window into 12 sub-PeakMaps (plus halo duplicates). With m/z-sorted spectra, bands could be VIEWS (index ranges per spectrum per band) instead of copies — MassTraceDetection takes a whole PeakMap though; one could pass per-band filtered... MTD needs contiguous spectra. Alternative: run MTD on the full window once with more internal parallelism — but MTD is serial. So copies are the price of banding. Could instead partition CompactFrames (cheap, 10 B/peak) and materialise per band... but materialize happens per window before banding. Restructure: materialize directly into band sub-maps from compact frames (band edges computed from compact m/z sample) — avoids the double materialisation. That's a real memory+time win: eliminates wmap entirely (saves ~2× compact transient and the copy time in trace).
  2091	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:162:  1. Frame summing before peak picking (the reference implementation core): sums ~5 adjacent cycles into composite (m/z,IM) frames → 5× fewer point observations to trace, and higher S/N means the noise threshold kills more junk → dramatically fewer fragment traces. speXtractor traces RAW frames at noise 10 → 8.5M traces/window. Trace count drives BOTH trace-stage and score-stage work superlinearly (score work ∝ precursors × candidate fragments).
  2092	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:163:  2. No MassTraceDetection-equivalent greedy m/z tracing: the reference implementation extracts fragment peaks from the summed matrix directly (RF peak picking) and correlates XICs — skips the per-apex candidate scan whose cost is superlinear in band density (the code comment at :2381-2383 admits MTD's per-apex candidate scan is superlinear: "halving a band halves the candidate list for every apex" — i.e. work ∝ N²/D... the very fact that banding reduces TOTAL CPU 11,168→2,972 s across 5→24 bands proves the tracing algorithm does redundant quadratic-ish work).
  2093	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:164:  3. Candidate gating before correlation: the reference implementation correlates only fragments passing its apex filters against the precursor's own elution window (few points); speXtractor scores every IM-band fragment against every precursor with support-length dot products, over 932k precursors including ~40% guessed singletons that mostly emit nothing (require_isotope_support doc: guessed spectra identify at 1.3%... but they still get SCORED). 40% of scoring work is on precursors that the reference implementation arguably never generates? Actually the reference implementation does assign default charges too. But: speXtractor emits per precursor HYPOTHESIS with fan-out 6.45 → the score loop covers all hypotheses.
  2094	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:170:  Wait — also LOAD: speXtractor spends 4,514 CPU-s on loading/picking (81.7x parallel — good), plus MS1 etc. the reference implementation total CPU 26,280 s vs speXtractor 82,440 s. If speXtractor's window-loop CPU were cut to match work volume... The score stage could be reduced by pre-filtering candidates with a cheap RT+IM index (2D grid) so the dot product runs only on pairs passing both gates — currently the IM-band scan iterates all fragments in IM band and checks RT per element: cheap per element but × 4.7e9... The dot product only runs for pairs passing RT gate. So the expensive part (support walk) is gated. The scan itself: 39k precursors × ~IM-band-size... if IM band holds ~5-15% of 8.5M = 400k-1.2M scans per precursor = 1.6e10-4.7e10 per window × 24 — that's 4e11-1.1e12 element visits just for the RT gate scan. At ~1e9/s/core that's 400-1100 CPU-s/core... hmm × how distributed. This is plausibly the dominant score cost — the linear scan over the IM band, not the dot product! Because RT gate kills most. So a 2D (IM,RT) index (sort fragments by IM, then bucket by RT within IM blocks, or a k-d/grid) would cut score work by ~the fraction passing RT gate (delta_rt=3 s over gradient ~1800 s → ~0.3% pass) → orders-of-magnitude fewer element visits: scan cost → candidates passing both gates ≈ 400k×0.003 ≈ 1,200 per precursor. Score could drop ~10-50×. THIS is a big mitigation: spatial index for the candidate loop at :1500-1505. Output-neutral if exact (same candidate set, same dot, same gates — just enumerated faster). Must preserve determinism: candidates processed per precursor independently; order of fi within a precursor affects nothing (emission collected in frags vector, sorted later by m/z in assembleFromList_ via sortByPosition; ties in partial_sort broken by m/z then index — index is position in frags vector! Line 1664-1665: tie-break `a < b` on index into frags — enumeration order matters for exact tie-breaks. Also pslot per-precursor deterministic. So the index must yield candidates in the same fi order — sort the bucket's indices by fi. Then output-identical. Falsify cheaply: diff mzML.
  2095	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:172:  Hmm wait, one more check on score stage cost: is the IM-band scan really dominant vs dot products? Fragment support ~510 points?? The comment says up to 510 support; a fragment trace spanning the whole gradient? min_sample_rate 0.5 and min_length 0... A fragment with 510 support points passing RT gate... RT gate is apex-RT within 3 s, so support points fall in a narrow RT range but grid-walk cost = support length regardless (walks ALL support, probing pdense). If avg support ~20-40 pts and ~1,200 surviving candidates/precursor: 39k × 1,200 × 30 = 1.4e9 ops/window vs scan 1.6e10. Scan dominates ~10×. Yes, the linear IM-band scan is the score hotspot. And score = 3,753.6 window-wall ≈ ~30-40k CPU-s. Cutting scan 10× cuts score CPU maybe 7-9× → saves ~25-30k CPU-s ≈ 30% of total CPU. Wall: score window-wall 3,753.6 → maybe ~600-800 → saves ~3,000/8 ≈ 370 s wall ≈ 27% of total wall (1,351 s). Biggest single neutral win. Risk: moderate (index construction per window, memory for index, exactness of ordering). Actually simpler alternative: since fragments are sorted by IM, build per-IM-block RT-sorted sub-index, or simply sort fragments by RT within coarse IM buckets... Fine, proposal stands: bucketed 2D index.
  2096	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:174:  Also trace stage (48.7% window time, superlinear MTD): mitigations: increase bands? Measured: 12 bands optimal wall (24 bands: more threads than cores). n_conc×n_bands=96 fixed. Could go 12 windows × 8 bands? Trace work falls with more bands superlinearly (CPU 11,168→5,378→2,972 for 5/12/24) — but wall limited by threads. With 24 windows: could do n_conc=4 × 24 bands = 96: trace CPU would drop a lot (2,972 vs 5,378 on dataset D = -45%)! But fewer windows in flight → worse tail/imbalance (only 4 slots for 24 skewed windows), admission memory pressure eases. The dataset D measurement says flat-24-bands loses on WALL despite lower CPU "because 24 windows × 24 bands = 576 threads on 100 cores" — but with n_conc=4 × 24 = 96 threads it's NOT oversubscribed! The 576-thread scenario was n_conc=8×24=192 or the old multiplication bug. The comment says "24 windows x 24 bands = 576 threads" — that was 24 concurrent windows (old cap without division). With proper division (n_conc = 100/24 = 4), 4×24=96. Trace CPU drops 45% but score's p_team = 100/4 = 25 (more per window — fine, score parallelises over precursors well) and windows in flight 4 → memory peak halves. But outer fill worsens: 24 windows / 4 slots with 136× skew → tail risk high; heavy-first helps. Worth an A/B: (n_conc, n_bands) ∈ {(8,12), (4,24), (12,8), (6,16)}. Cheap to test via existing perf:trace_bands option (pins all windows to N bands, and n_conc auto-derives). Output-neutral (band partition is exact). Expected gain: if trace CPU halves (53.6k→~30k on this file's mix) and wall is trace-dominated... rough: window-loop CPU 77.3k → ~55k; wall 1139 → maybe 850-950 s. Uncertain; flag as experiment, medium risk (tail imbalance could eat gains).
  2097	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:176:  Hmm, but careful: the dataset D numbers: trace CPU falls 11,168 → 5,378 → 2,972 s for 5/12/24 bands. So on dataset D, going 12→24 bands halves trace CPU. If the same holds on dataset A: trace CPU maybe ~45k → ~25k. Total window CPU 77.3k → 57k. If wall scaled with CPU at same fill, 1139 → 840 s (-26%). But tail risk at 4 concurrent windows. Also p_team=25 per window for score — score inner regions use 25 threads each; fill fine.
  2098	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:178:  Question 1 binding constraint answer: The binding constraint is the correlation kernel's raw work volume + trace stage raw work (they're 95% of window-time), NOT the concurrency cap: the grid is 96/100 threads and even a perfect scheduler only recovers the 29% idle → best-case wall ~1139×0.71≈810 s for the loop; total ~1,020 s vs the reference implementation 586 s. So scheduling alone cannot close the 2.3× wall gap; work reduction must come from the algorithm side. Which numbers would have to be wrong: the stage sums (8,139.5) and window-loop CPU (77,333). If the 67.9x already reflected max achievable... The claim "29% recoverable by scheduling" fails if: (a) the admission/tail idle (972 outer-s) is actually forced by memory (admission serialized because budget < need — then it's a memory constraint, not scheduling; settle by logging admit-wait vs window count; if waits dominate, raising perf:mem_fraction or reducing per-window footprint is the fix, not scheduling); (b) if the 12× reservation model overstates (e.g., memory-bandwidth saturation: 96 threads streaming FragGrid at ~35 GB/window could be bandwidth-bound, making "idle" threads actually stalled on memory — settle with perf stat: measure memory bandwidth / stalled cycles during score stage; if bandwidth-saturated, the score index fix helps doubly by shrinking the working set).
  2099	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:180:  Also self-contradiction check on user numbers: "mean cores busy 60 of 128" overall vs LOAD 81.7x + window 67.9x weighted: (55.2×81.7 + 83×5 + 50.6×1 + 1139×67.9 + 2×1 + 21.1×1)/1351 = (4510+415+50.6+77339+2+21.1)/1351 = 82,338/1351 = 60.9. ✓ consistent with 60. And 22.9 h CPU = 82,440 s ✓ ≈ 82,338. Consistent.
