  2110	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:215:  But hold on — this assumes trace CPU really halves on dataset A with 24 bands (dataset D measurement) and that admission/fill behave. And 24 bands × halo on narrow windows: comment says narrow tile (19 Da) at 24 bands = 57% halo overhead — but that overhead is included in the dataset D CPU measurement presumably (dataset D also has 24 windows...). The per-window width-derived band heuristic was REJECTED in favour of flat 12 (:2376-2380). Flat 24 lost on WALL at 8×24=192 threads oversubscribed — but with n_conc=4 it's 96 threads. The existing option pins bands and auto-derives n_conc = 100/24 = 4. So the A/B is literally `-perf:trace_bands 24`. Also intermediate: 6×16=96. Test all.
  2111	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:217:  3. **Parallelise buildFragGrid + materialise directly into bands (skip wmap)** — fraggrid 396 s serial window-wall (~4.6% loop, and 4,354 s inner-idle capacity). Parallelise the per-fragment support indexing (lower_bounds independent per fragment; grid union via parallel merge or just build per-thread then merge). Gain: ~350 window-wall → ~45 s wall + reduced idle. Also restructure materialise→bands: saves ~2× compact transient copy time inside trace stage (part of trace's serial partition) and ~2 GB×8 memory. Output-neutral. Risk: low-medium (must preserve exact edge quantiles and CSR order — flat order = fragment order = sorted by (im,mz,rt,intensity)... wait, frag_traces sorted AFTER detectTraces_ at :2411, then buildFragGrid on sorted order. CSR entries per fragment in xic order — deterministic. Parallel build with per-fragment segments into precomputed offsets is deterministic.)
  2112	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:219:  4. **Fix the uint32_t truncation bug at :731/:742** (g.off entries cast to uint32_t despite uint64_t vector and comment claiming 64-bit) — correctness, not perf, but hostile review must flag: on the widest windows the design itself says we're within 2× of wrap; one denser file (lower ms2 noise) crosses it and every correlation silently reads wrong support spans. Zero-risk fix: remove the cast. (And g.off[i] assigned per fragment — sentinel too.) Actually also check: `g.off.resize(frags.size()+1, 0)` uint64; `supBegin returns off[i]` fine. The (uint32_t) casts truncate. Must fix. Also `int gi` grid index int — G < 2^31 fine.
  2113	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:221:  5. **Admission estimate recalibration** — 10× under-covers measured ~14× avg; raise multiplier or make it self-tuning from measured per-window RSS deltas. Prevents OOM on shared/smaller nodes; may reduce concurrency slightly here (memory headroom already allows 8). Neutral-to-none on wall for this run; risk low. Gain: robustness, and IF admission waits are found nonzero (item: measure admit-wait), then either the multiplier is too HIGH for big windows or memory-bound — measurement first: log per-window admit wait.
  2114	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:225:  7. **Reduce trace work at the source (algorithmic, output-changing)**: native pre-centroid frame aggregation (trace:native_ms2_neighbors) — the reference implementation's frame summing; cuts trace input volume ~5× and trace count; the code comment says tool-level aggregation lost 26% peptides, native untested/ungated. This is the one that could close the the reference implementation work gap but CHANGES OUTPUT → hard sell under peptide-set gate. Mention as the honest answer to "can we reach the reference implementation": only via output-changing algorithm alignment.
  2115	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:236:  - admit-wait time per window (distinguish scheduling tail vs memory serialization): add a timer around the for(;;) loop. Cheap.
  2116	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:237:  - per-stage CPU not just window-wall (trace vs score fill): use clock_gettime(CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID) per band/chunk, or getrusage deltas can't isolate; simplest: per-band wall times logged (already partially via span_log? No). Log per-band duration in detectTraces_ and per-chunk in score.
  2117	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:238:  - memory-bandwidth saturation during score: perf stat -e cycles,instructions,cache-misses on a score-heavy window; or run one window alone and see if score fill improves (if fill rises with fewer concurrent windows → bandwidth/contention; if unchanged → algorithmic imbalance).
  2118	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:239:  - peak RSS attribution: per-window-stage RSS logs (code has rss_() at window done only) — add RSS read at trace/grid/score stage ends for the widest window.
  2119	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:242:  (a) The source comment at :2274-2276 claims trace=69.34%, score=28.81% of window-loop CPU (dataset B/dataset D), but THIS run's stage wall sums are trace 48.7% / score 46.1%. If the CPU split still held, trace CPU = 0.693×77,333 = 53.6k s vs max possible 12×3,965.6 = 47.6k — impossible. So either the split shifted (score became relatively heavier — plausible: different file, thresholds) or timers inconsistent. Not a contradiction of the USER's numbers per se, but shows the old 69/29 model is dead; the missing measurement is per-stage CPU.
  2120	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:246:  (c) The user says "an empty pipeline always admits one window regardless of size" and "heaviest-first" — with heavy-first, the FIRST admitted window is the largest; since pipeline starts empty it's admitted regardless of budget. Fine.
  2121	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:248:  (d) User's claim "12 GB per window in flight" vs admission need avg 8.9 GB: inflight accounting would cap at budget/need ≈ more windows than 8? n_conc=8 caps anyway. Fine.
  2122	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:250:  (e) "peak RSS 151.7, loop adds ~97 GB" — with 8 in flight and ~12 GB each. But if the widest window's FragGrid is 35 GB, 8×12 avg is misleading; peak may be base+7 small windows×~8 + 1 big×40. Consistent numerically (55+56+40=151). Note the peak likely occurs when the widest window overlaps 7 others — heavy-first starts it at t=0, so peak early in loop. Plausible.
  2123	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:252:  Let me also double check the trace stage: is EPD included in t_trace? Yes — detectTraces_ includes EPD split (:1041-1062) within _t2.._t3. EPD known dominant serial cost; it's parallelised num_threads(bands) over 4×bands chunks. With 24 bands → 96 chunks, num_threads(24) → better fill. Consistent with rebalance recommendation.
  2124	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:254:  One more possible CPU sink: `#pragma omp critical` per window-done log (:2400) — trivial.
  2125	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:256:  And GOMP spin: with OMP_WAIT_POLICY unset, libgomp spins GOMP_SPINCOUNT (~300k) before sleeping — at 8×12 nested teams forming/completing thousands of times (24 windows × 2 inner regions + EPD regions + band loops: ~24×3=72 region entries, negligible). Barrier waits within imbalanced regions: threads sleep after ~1 ms spinning — negligible CPU. OK, not a major factor; mention only briefly? Skip or one line. Actually inner idle 15.7k s is mostly genuine imbalance (waiting at barrier, sleeping), not spin. Fine.
  2126	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:258:  Also `schedule(dynamic)` on band loop at :1000 with 12 bands over 12 threads — one band = one chunk; if one band is 3× the median, the trace stage wall = that band. Quantile bands equalize PEAK COUNT, not trace work; work per peak varies with local intensity/noise. Suggest: more bands than threads per window for the band loop? bands=12 with num_threads(12) → dynamic can't rebalance. Could do 24 sub-bands over 12 threads (2 chunks each) → better balance but more halo. Or make score-style chunking. Minor vs grid A/B.
  2127	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:262:  Now Q1 "which of my numbers would have to be wrong for your explanation to fail": My decomposition rests on: stage wall sums (8,139.5) ≈ outer-busy time; CPU 77,333; wall 1,139. If stage sums were actually CPU-s (mislabeled) or if admit-waits are large (memory-bound rather than scheduling), the conclusion "inner imbalance 14.4% + tail/admission 10.7% + serial 4.2%" shifts. Specifically: if admit-wait ≈ 0 (measurable), then the 972 s outer gap is pure tail/heavy-first granularity → window rebalance won't help, only smaller work units. If per-band timings show balance, then inner idle is memory-bandwidth stalls → the score index/footprint fixes matter more than scheduling. Ask for: per-window admit-wait, per-band wall, per-stage CPU.
  2128	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:265:  1. **Input-volume to the tracer**: the reference implementation sums adjacent cycles pre-centroid (S/N ↑ ~√N, point count ↓ ~N×) so its peak-picking/trace stage sees ~1/5 the points and far fewer noise traces. speXtractor traces every raw frame at noise 10 → 2.19e9 peaks compacted, 8.5M traces in big windows. Trace+score are ~95% of window CPU; both scale (super)linearly with trace count. This alone can be ~2-3×.
  2129	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:266:  2. **Superlinear m/z tracer**: MTD's per-apex candidate scan makes tracing superlinear in local peak density (proven by own measurement: banding cuts TOTAL CPU 11,168→2,972 s from 5→24 bands — a linear algorithm would show flat CPU). the reference implementation's matrix-extraction approach doesn't pay this.
  2130	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:267:  3. **Correlation candidate volume**: every precursor × every fragment in the IM band, linear-scanned then support-walked; ~1M precursor hypotheses incl. ~40% guessed singletons + fan-out 6.45 share-all. the reference implementation correlates per detected precursor FEATURE with intensity-ranked fragment sets and retains top-500 by intensity — fewer, cheaper pairs. Also RT gating by apex distance only after full scan.
  2131	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:268:  4. **Redundant halo work**: 12 bands × 20 ppm halo + 1 Da window overlap → duplicated tracing at every edge (~12% + ~2%).
  2132	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:269:  5. Valley-splitting EPD over 8.5M traces/window (+38% wall documented) — the reference implementation doesn't split OpenMS-style traces because it doesn't build them.
  2133	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:276:  - detectTraces_ serial prologue: quantile sampling (:955-965: iterate all spectra collecting every 97th peak m/z, sort) + serial partition copy of ~all MS1 peaks into 12 sub-PeakMaps (:969-997) — this is a full O(peaks×log?) copy on ONE thread. MS1 = 37% of raw peaks... after picking maybe ~10-15% of 2.19e9 = 2-3e8 peaks × ~30 ns ≈ 10-20 s serial.
  2134	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:297:  R6. Admission self-calibration (measure actual per-window RSS delta; multiplier 10→~14 or adaptive) — robustness; tiny wall effect here; prevents OOM-driven serialization elsewhere; add admit-wait timing to settle whether item-2 losses are tail or memory.
  2135	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:299:  R7. Algorithmic/output-changing (hard sell, gated on peptide-set equality): native pre-centroid aggregation (trace:native_ms2_neighbors), which is the real the reference implementation-work-gap closer; requires re-validation on the benchmark gate. State plainly: with output fixed, ceiling is ~1,351 → ~750-850 s (1.6-1.8× vs the reference implementation's 586), not parity; closing the remaining ~1.4× needs the algorithm changes (frame summing + fewer traces + tighter candidate generation) that change output.
  2136	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:303:  Also: memory peak driver statement: the peak-driving allocation is most plausibly the widest window's (FragGrid.flat + still-live Trace XICs + sub[] band copies + MTD trace store) transient — during buildFragGrid, xic (16 B/pt) and flat (8 B/pt) coexist: 24 B/pt; if the wide window has ~4e8-4e9 support points → 10-90 GB. Given measured +97 GB total, and the compact store for the wide window ~5 GB, a 24 B/pt structure with 5 GB×(1 pt per ~10 B... compact peaks ≈ support points lower bound... support points ≤ peaks. Wide window peaks ≈ 5e8 (5 GB compact at 10 B) → XIC 8 GB + flat 4 GB + wmap 10 GB + sub 10 GB... these don't all coexist: wmap freed at :998 (before MTD completes? map.clear(true) at :998 happens right after partition, BEFORE band tracing — so during band tracing: sub[] (12 maps, ~10 GB incl halo) + growing MTD traces. After: traces+XICs (~16+ GB), then +flat (~8 GB) while xic alive. Peak per wide window ~25-35 GB. So peak total 151.7 = 55 base + ~7 avg windows × 9-10 + wide-window transient ~30 ≈ 150. ✓ Consistent. So the peak driver = widest window's trace-phase working set (sub copies + MTD traces + XIC + grid). Good.
  2137	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:305:  Is 10× + 256 MB defensible? For the wide window: need = 50 GB vs actual transient ~30 GB → over-covered; for average window: need 8.9 vs actual ~10-12 → under-covered. It's a crude linear model; direction fine, calibration ±40%. The bigger issue: `inflight` is charged `need` for the WHOLE window lifetime, but actual footprint swings ~4× between phases; and admission re-reads MemAvailable which includes page cache — on a 128-core shared node with big page cache, MemAvailable is optimistic. It's defensible as a heuristic but not as a guarantee; the measured 97 GB delta vs 8×8.9=71 GB booked proves the booking under-covers by ~35% in practice. Recommend self-calibrating multiplier (measure RSS delta per window, adapt) — cheap, keeps shared-node safety.
  2138	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:307:  One more check on the emit path: pslot pre-sized per precursor (:2518) — 39k MSSpectrum objects per window ~ fine.
  2139	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:315:  And "windows processed heaviest-first" — :2253 stable_sort by wbytes desc, schedule(dynamic) → first 8 iterations start immediately (the 8 biggest). ✓.
  2140	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:322:  1. Band partition copies all peaks into sub[] (:969-997) instead of materialising compact→bands directly (compact already partitioned by m/z quantiles possible). Double transient: wmap + sub coexist briefly... actually sub[b] accumulates while wmap fully alive; then wmap cleared. Peak = wmap + all sub ≈ 2× window peaks.
  2141	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:324:  3. toTrace copies MassTrace→Trace (XIC copy) while mts alive until end of detectTraces_ — the per-band merge at :1016-1018 moves, ok; but `mts` and `out` coexist: another 1.5-2× on trace data.
  2142	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:327:  So per-window transient inflation ~3-4× over minimal — that's where the 12 GB comes from. The admission model (10×) roughly prices this.
  2143	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:333:  Also small: `schedule(dynamic)` outer loop with admission INSIDE the iteration means a thread holding a big window blocks others from... no, dynamic gives each thread next window when free; admission may make a thread wait while others could take smaller windows that fit — head-of-line blocking: a thread stuck waiting for a 50 GB need blocks its slot even though a later small window would fit. With heavy-first + dynamic, iteration order is fixed (heavy→light); waiting threads each hold a specific wi. Minor: could re-order to "admit any window that fits" (work-stealing by size). Small gain; mention under R6.
  2144	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:351:  - scoreCandidates_ IM-band scan :1500-1505; RT gate :1505; dot loop :1521-1522; overlap guard :1523; min_pts skip :1520
  2145	docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:357:  - materializeWindow :467-497; frees frames :491-495
