  1312	  /// [wavelet] Stationary ("a trous") B3-spline wavelet smoothing of an elution profile.
  1313	  ///
  1314	  /// WHY. the reference implementation correlates a SMOOTHED precursor XIC against RAW fragment XICs (Nat Commun
  1315	  /// 16:95, Methods: 2D Gaussian pre-filter, then Savitzky-Golay for elongated peaks). We copied
  1316	  /// its 0.3 correlation threshold WITHOUT copying the smoothing. Pearson r between two noisy
  1317	  /// profiles is attenuated toward 0 as S/N falls, so an unsmoothed 0.3 is a systematically weaker
  1318	  /// constraint than a smoothed 0.3 -- and the attenuation is worst exactly at faint precursors,
  1319	  /// which is where the coverage analysis says we lose peptides (MISS precursors are 3.2x dimmer).
  1320	  /// Raising the threshold to compensate was tried and produced only an FDR artefact; smoothing
  1321	  /// attacks the cause instead of the symptom.
  1322	  ///
  1323	  /// A-TROUS, not a decimated DWT: no downsampling, so the output stays sample-aligned with the
  1324	  /// input and can be scattered onto the correlation grid at the SAME RT positions. That matters
  1325	  /// for correctness here, not just convenience -- the overlap gate counts grid points where the
  1326	  /// precursor is non-zero, so a transform that moved or resampled points would silently change
  1327	  /// what `min_correlation_points` means.
  1328	  ///
  1329	  /// B3-spline scaling filter [1,4,6,4,1]/16, dilated by 2^(j-1) at level j; c_J is a low-pass of
  1330	  /// support ~2^J samples. J comes from the MEASURED MS1 FWHM (see atrousLevels), never a guess.
  1331	  void atrousSmooth(const vector<double>& in, int levels, vector<double>& out)
  1332	  {
  1333	    static const double H[5] = {1.0 / 16, 4.0 / 16, 6.0 / 16, 4.0 / 16, 1.0 / 16};
  1334	    const int n = (int)in.size();
  1335	    out = in;
  1336	    if (n < 3 || levels <= 0) return;
  1337	    vector<double> tmp(n);
  1338	    for (int j = 1; j <= levels; ++j)
  1339	    {
  1340	      const int step = 1 << (j - 1);
  1341	      for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i)
  1342	      {
  1343	        double acc = 0.0;
  1344	        for (int k = -2; k <= 2; ++k)
  1345	        {
  1346	          int idx = i + k * step;
