   516	  /// the instrument's own, and a coarse approximation of the wrong axis. This tool only runs on
   517	  /// ion-mobility TOF data, so the native axis is always available and there is no reason to
   518	  /// approximate it.
   519	  ///
   520	  /// Why the native axis is better than a uniform m/z grid, not merely smaller:
   521	  ///   * it is LOSSLESS -- the bin IS the measurement, so no quantum has to be justified;
   522	  ///   * ~5e5 bins instead of ~1.7e8 grid points, so a bin index is a usable array subscript;
   523	  ///   * the SAME ion gives the SAME bin in every frame, while its calibrated m/z wobbles with the
   524	  ///     digitizer temperature -- so grouping in TOF space does not have to spend part of its ppm
   525	  ///     tolerance absorbing calibration drift, which is exactly what the m/z-space tolerance does;
