   827	  /// docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/ and the corrected semantics are:
   828	  ///   * seeds in intensity order, ties later-index-first (stable ascending sort, reverse walk);
   829	  ///   * the two directions extend INTERLEAVED, one frame down then one up, sharing one centroid;
   830	  ///   * acceptance is the CLOSEST peak within +-3 sd of the running m/z centroid and within the
   831	  ///     mobility tolerance of the running mobility centroid; if that closest peak is already
   832	  ///     taken, the frame is a MISS -- the next-closest is not tried;
   833	  ///   * sd starts at centroid*ppm and is RE-ESTIMATED from the accepted peaks (reestimate_mt_sd);
   834	  ///   * a direction stops after more than 5 consecutive misses; an empty frame is not a miss;
   835	  ///   * the trace is valid if its RT span is within [min,max] length and
   836	  ///     size / (frames visited - trailing misses) >= min_sample_rate (0.5 by default);
   837	  ///   * peaks are marked taken only when the trace is valid;
   838	  ///   * the seed is counted twice in the centroid, as run_ does (init, then update again).
   839	  /// Peaks stay integers (flight-time bin, quantised mobility); the running centroid and sd are
   840	  /// doubles in m/z, exactly as in OpenMS, so the tolerance semantics are the same. The bin<->m/z
   841	  /// conversion is one calibration call per frame step plus one per ACCEPTED peak -- never per
   842	  /// visited peak. Valley splitting runs afterwards on the result, as it does for the OpenMS path.
   843	  /// Per-window, read-only preparation shared by every band task: frame of each peak, the
   844	  /// noise-membership mask, per-frame "has any member" (OpenMS deletes sub-noise peaks first and
   845	  /// only then asks whether a frame is empty), and the intensity-ordered seed list. Built ONCE.
   846	  /// The first corrected version rebuilt all of this inside each of the 12 band tasks -- twelve
   847	  /// full-window sorts per window and twelve sets of P-sized arrays alive at once -- which is
   848	  /// exactly where its extra time (9:28 vs 7:22) and extra memory (174 vs 166 GB) came from.
   849	  struct TracePrep
   850	  {
   851	    vector<char>     frame_live;  ///< F: has at least one member
