commit 0e518a48b173236df8a5bb4991dd7d3c2c535a7c
Author:     Oliver <oliver.kohlbacher@uni-tuebingen.de>
AuthorDate: Thu Sep 3 17:58:31 2026 +0200
Commit:     Oliver <oliver.kohlbacher@uni-tuebingen.de>
CommitDate: Thu Sep 3 17:58:31 2026 +0200

    Data structures: one global discrete RT axis; traces store frame INDEX + intensity (8 B/point, was 16), profile alignment becomes integer equality
    
    The retention-time axis is discrete -- one value per acquired frame -- and every trace point sits on
    a frame, so an RT never needed storing per point. It was stored ~10^8 times per window and every
    alignment of two profiles cost a binary search because the values had to be compared numerically.
    
    - vector<double> rtAxis(): built once from every loaded frame, read-only thereafter.
    - Trace: vector<uint32_t> xi + vector<float> xv instead of vector<pair<double,double>>; accessors
      np()/rtAt()/intAt()/freeProfile() so callers never see the layout.
    - buildFragGrid: marks touched frames in a flat array and numbers them, so axis index -> grid slot
      is one array read. No collecting of 10^8 RT values, no large sort, no per-point binary search.
      G (the Pearson denominator) is still the count of touched frames, so the arithmetic is unchanged.
    - The precursor-to-fragment-frame nearest match within gate:delta_rt is precomputed ONCE per window
      (it depends only on the window) instead of being re-derived per precursor per point.
    - xicCorr: an integer merge of two index lists, no tolerance and no search.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
