codex
Two concrete defects are now confirmed, not hypothetical: `e3c6eb4` claims to make the integer detector the default but never changes the registered default (`"openms"` remains), and the “corrected” ledger still starts tracking the trace arena only after valley splitting, missing the parent/bins/child-arena coexistence it is supposed to measure. I’m checking release blockers and the exact validation matrix next.
exec
/bin/bash -lc "nl -ba docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/FAILURE-ANALYSIS.md | sed -n '110,190p'; nl -ba docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/TRIAGE.md; nl -ba docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.md | sed -n '1,190p'; nl -ba docs/reviews/day-assessment-2026-09-04/codex.transcript | tail -n 260" in /Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor
 succeeded in 0ms:
   110	
   111	**Memory is the bigger and more consistent result: 140 GB against 230 GB, a 39% cut**, matching the
   112	36% seen on dataset D (105 vs 164 GB). It also puts speXtractor BELOW the reference implementation's 163 GB on this file for
   113	the first time. Wall time is a wash here (11:51 vs 11:42) although it won on dataset D (6:53 vs 7:17);
   114	window-loop CPU is 31,033 s against 41,530 s, i.e. the integer path does 25% less work but fills
   115	fewer threads (64x vs 87x), so on the larger file the two effects cancel.
   116	
   117	Remaining before it could be a default: MSFragger and entrapment (queued), and an explanation for
   118	the occupancy gap -- 64x vs 87x is the last unexplained number in this line of work.
   119	
   120	
   121	## Both engines and entrapment (2026-09-03, 23:41)
   122	
   123	| dataset D | Sage @1% | MSFragger @1% | entrapment FDR |
   124	|---|---|---|---|
   125	| OpenMS detector (shipping default) | 12,642 | 12,073 | 1.38% [1.15-1.64] |
   126	| integer detector | 12,466-12,475 (−1.3%) | **12,328 (+2.1%)** | 1.24% [1.01-1.50] |
   127	
   128	**The two engines disagree in sign.** Sage loses 1.3%, MSFragger gains 2.1%, on the same spectra --
   129	and on dataset A Sage itself gained 0.3%. So there is no evidence of a systematic loss; there is evidence
   130	of a per-engine, per-file wobble of about a percent either way, which is what a faithful-but-not-
   131	identical reimplementation produces. Entrapment is slightly LOWER (1.24% vs 1.38%), so nothing here
   132	is bought with false positives.
   133	
   134	**The candidate-metric revert did essentially nothing:** 12,466 -> 12,475 on Sage (+9), 12,328 ->
   135	12,322 on MSFragger (−6). So the earlier attribution of the 12,650 -> 12,466 move to the bin metric
   136	was WRONG too -- neither that nor the frame-emptiness rule explains it, and both A/Bs came back
   137	null. The remaining difference between those two builds is the per-window preparation hoist and the
   138	chunked valley splitting, i.e. the parallel structure, which means the ~1% is scheduling-order
   139	sensitivity in a greedy consume-and-mark algorithm, not a semantic difference. That is expected and
   140	is the reason this project gates on peptide SETS rather than counts.
   141	
   142	**Status: the adoption conditions are met on everything except a reason to prefer it.** Both engines
   143	within ~2% either way, entrapment no worse, dataset A replicated on memory (−39%) and peptides (+0.3%).
   144	The case for it is memory (−36 to −39%, and below the reference implementation for the first time) and CPU (−25%); the
   145	case against is a 64x vs 87x occupancy gap that is still unexplained, and that gap is what makes the
   146	wall-time win vanish on the larger file.
     1	# Adversarial review triage, 2026-09-03 (codex + kimi + vibe)
     2	
     3	Prompt: `docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/` (five questions: correctness, code-vs-doc consistency,
     4	package completeness, internal commentary, test coverage). All three reviewers returned substantive
     5	reviews (codex 145 lines, kimi 115, vibe 787) -- no SILENT reviewer, so the yield gate passed.
     6	
     7	Both codex and kimi independently cleared the thing most likely to be wrong: **no data race in the
     8	OpenMP window loop or the parallel MS1 pick.** Picker instances are `firstprivate`, per-window and
     9	per-precursor output slots are disjoint, shared appends are guarded. That is two independent clears
    10	on the code that was rewritten for parallelism, and it is the most valuable single result here.
    11	
    12	## Fixed in this pass
    13	
    14	| # | finding | reviewers | fix |
    15	|---|---|---|---|
    16	| 1 | MS1 traces were never filtered for non-finite coordinates, though fragment traces were. A NaN makes the sort comparator non-transitive (UB) and corrupts every later binary search. | codex, kimi | filter + log before the canonical sort |
    17	| 2 | `FragGrid::off` CSR offsets were `uint32_t`; 8.5 M fragments x ~510 support points is within 2x of wrapping, and overflow silently corrupts every correlation. | codex, kimi | widened to `uint64_t` |
    18	| 3 | Negative counts wrapped through `Size` (`min_fragments -1` -> ~1.8e19, nothing emitted) and negative tolerances gated everything out. Both failed as an empty but successful run. | codex, kimi | 17 `setMinInt_`/`setMinFloat_`/`setMaxFloat_` bounds; `0 = auto` preserved for both band counts |
    19	| 4 | mzPeak: an unknown `frame_id` silently substituted the reference T1, so masses calibrated at the wrong temperature were stamped exact -- and the provenance record vouched for them. | codex | throws; also rejects non-positive input m/z instead of `llround(NaN)` |
    20	| 5 | mzPeak: a short `get_spectra_batch` result was clamped with `min()`, leaving empty tail frames and a successful run. | codex | throws on any size mismatch |
    21	| 6 | mzPeak: m/z and intensity arrays truncated to the shorter; a short IM array became "no IM". | codex | throws on mismatch |
    22	| 7 | **Shipped defaults could not reproduce any published number**: every figure used `ms2_noise_threshold_int 10` and `ms2_split_valleys 7.0` on the command line while the registered defaults were 100 and 0. | codex, kimi | defaults moved to the benchmarked values; the "Current defaults" block in dataset D-BASELINE now lists all ten and says it was verified against the code |
    23	| 8 | `SPEXTRACTOR_MZ_ESTIMATOR` set every reported precursor and fragment m/z from an undocumented env var, invisible in `--help`. | kimi | registered as `trace:mz_estimator` (apex/mean/median, default apex) with valid strings |
    24	| 9 | `trace:split_valleys_fwhm` help claimed it overrides `ms1_split_valleys`; the code modifies `ms2_split` only. | codex | help corrected |
    25	| 10 | `charge:ambiguity_margin` is unreachable in the default `count` scoring mode. | codex, kimi | help states the mode dependency |
    26	| 11 | CHANGELOG advertised `SPEXTRACTOR_ISO_COLLAPSE` and the backfill env vars, which the ponytail pass had just removed. | codex, kimi | changelog rewritten to match the code, with Fixed/Removed sections |
    27	| 12 | Doxygen header sent readers to `PLAN.md` / `REVIEW.md`, neither of which ships. | codex, kimi | points at `docs/dataset D-BASELINE.md` and `docs/reviews/` |
    28	| 13 | `MzPeakStreamLoad.h` header said the exact calibration is enabled with `=1`; it is default-on and `=0` disables. | codex | comment corrected |
    29	| 14 | `bands_for`, a per-window band heuristic, was defined, never called, and a comment said it was kept for reference. | codex | removed |
    30	| 15 | `ponytail:` as a code tag has no meaning outside this project. | codex | reworded to `deliberate:` |
    31	| 16 | README called both 15:21 and 12:49 "the current runtime", and its two determinism claims read as contradictory. | codex | runtime updated to 12:49/102 GB; the fixed-binary and cross-build claims separated explicitly |
    32	| 17 | No test suite at all; verification was whole-file cluster runs only. | codex, kimi, vibe | `test/test_spextractor.py`: six checks on a synthetic input -- isotope ownership, min_charge in both directions, two parameter-rejection checks, and 1-vs-4-thread byte identity |
    33	
    34	## Rejected, with evidence
    35	
    36	**"Precursors on a shared window boundary are emitted twice" (codex, HIGH).** Not a bug. Measured
    37	on the dataset D output: all 23 adjacent isolation-window pairs overlap by exactly 1.000 Da, the standard
    38	diaPASEF overlap. A precursor in an overlap band is genuinely isolated by both windows and yields two
    39	independent measurements, so inclusive bounds on both edges are correct. The signature is visible and
    40	benign: 27,440 of 927,813 spectra share an exact (m/z, RT, IM, charge) tuple, always with
    41	multiplicity exactly 2, never 3. Recorded here so it is not re-flagged.
    42	
    43	## Accepted, not yet fixed
    44	
    45	These are real and are release work, not code defects:
    46	
    47	- **Externally reproducible benchmark (codex BLOCKER).** The three files are patient-derived and
    48	  cannot be published. A public PXD replication was already the open C10 item; v0.3.0 should ship
    49	  with one public dataset reproduced end to end, or state plainly that the numbers are not
    50	  independently checkable.
    51	- **Build instructions and dependency identity (codex HIGH).** No OpenMS or mzPeak commit SHA,
    52	  compiler version, or working standalone recipe. The README also implies a serial build works while
    53	  the source calls OpenMP functions directly.
    54	- **Resource requirements (codex HIGH).** 100-165 GB peak RSS is not stated anywhere a user would
    55	  look, and the admission logic deliberately admits one oversized window.
    56	- **Input/output contract (codex MEDIUM).** Required mzML content, per-peak mobility arrays,
    57	  isolation-window offsets, the `spx_guessed` / `spx_n_isotopes` metadata and charge-0 behaviour are
    58	  undocumented.
    59	- **`consolidate` was falsified with a dead IM gate (kimi S2).** The code comment admits
    60	  `getDriftTime()` returned -1 throughout, so the mobility gate never fired. That falsification is
    61	  not evidence about the lever as designed and should be re-run or downgraded to "untested".
    62	- **Full help-string compression (codex/kimi, question 4).** Only the three worst were done. The
    63	  remaining `FALSIFIED` help texts still carry per-sample counts and dated arm narratives; the
    64	  agreed target form is "Experimental; default off. Lost X, see docs/...". Source comments carrying
    65	  route/arm chronology should move to `docs/experiments/` with stable experiment IDs.
    66	- **Remaining test items.** Codex's window-partition, `aggregateFrames_` consumed-peak,
    67	  band-equivalence and mzPeak contract tests need either a test-only compilation mode or fixture
    68	  archives; the six shipped checks do not cover them.
     1	The current working tree is not ready for a public v0.3.0 release. The worst problems are silent mzPeak corruption/omission paths, known isotope-walk misassignment, misleading “open-search safe” behavior, and the absence of tests for any of this core logic.
     2	
     3	## 1. Correctness and safety of the code
     4	
     5	- **BLOCKER — mixed or wrong calibration is stamped as exact.** [`MzPeakStreamLoad.h:167`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/MzPeakStreamLoad.h:167>), `MzPeakExactMz::exact()`: a missing/out-of-range `frame_id` silently substitutes `cal.T1_ref`; a non-finite recalculated value silently leaves the archive’s approximate m/z in place. The output is nevertheless stamped `tdf_table_modeltype1`. `sqrt(v)`/`llround()` is also unsafe for invalid input. Smallest fix: require a valid `Frames.Id` and explicit T1 entry for every frame; reject non-finite/non-positive input or calibrated m/z instead of retaining the old value.
     6	
     7	- **BLOCKER — the mzPeak batch loader silently drops frames.** [`MzPeakStreamLoad.h:335`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/MzPeakStreamLoad.h:335>), `get_spectra_batch()`: the loop stops at `min(want.size(), got.size())`; a short library result leaves empty slots that are quietly skipped at line 348. That produces incomplete mzML with a successful exit. Smallest fix: require `got.size() == want.size()` and verify returned IDs/order before conversion; otherwise throw.
     8	
     9	- **BLOCKER — malformed or unsupported MS2 input can yield a valid-looking empty/partial result.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1787`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1787>), [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1838`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1838>), [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2850`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2850>): validation checks only that MS1 has some IM data. MS2 frames without IM or precursors are silently skipped, zero windows are allowed, and zero emitted spectra are written with `EXECUTION_OK`. Smallest fix: count and reject missing-IM/missing-window MS2 frames, require at least one usable isolation window, and fail if all windows or all output spectra disappear.
    10	
    11	- **BLOCKER — the default contiguous isotope walk is a known wrong-mass generator.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1163`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1163>), `inferPrecursors_()`: the first missing isotope terminates each direction. Higher isotopes are then left unmarked and can seed new precursors with an M+1/M+2 monoisotope. The project’s own baseline confirms this mechanism at `dataset D-BASELINE.md:703-713`. Smallest fix: separate scoring from ownership—scan the bounded isotope lattice for the selected charge despite gaps, then mark every matched member of the selected envelope as consumed. Rebenchmark before adoption.
    12	
    13	- **HIGH — isotope matching chooses the lowest-m/z candidate, not the best candidate.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1132`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1132>), `findPartner`: it returns the first trace in the tolerance interval. With multiple co-eluting candidates, that is neither nearest in ppm nor best in RT, IM, XIC correlation, or intensity. It deterministically returns the wrong trace. Smallest fix: evaluate the whole interval and minimize a documented tuple such as absolute ppm error, RT error, IM error, then stable trace ID.
    14	
    15	- **HIGH — window identity is lossy.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1723`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1723>) and [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:602`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:602>): windows are keyed only by m/z bounds rounded to 0.01 Da; mobility bands and `swath_nr` are discarded. Distinct windows can be merged, particularly methods reusing an m/z interval at different mobility bands. Smallest fix: use a structured acquisition-window identity containing unrounded/canonically quantized m/z and IM bounds, and abort on inconsistent collisions.
    16	
    17	- **HIGH — precursors on a shared window boundary are emitted twice.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2394`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2394>): `lower_bound(win_lo)` plus `upper_bound(win_hi)` makes both edges inclusive. Adjacent windows `[a,b]` and `[b,c]` both claim a precursor exactly at `b`. Smallest fix: use half-open intervals `[lo,hi)`, with an explicit exception only for the final acquisition boundary.
    18	
    19	- **HIGH — non-finite MS1 traces enter sort and binary-search code unchecked.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1998`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1998>) and `inferPrecursors_`: fragments are filtered for finite coordinates, but MS1 traces are not. NaNs invalidate the comparator and the sorted-range precondition required by `lower_bound`. The comment at line 2392 claiming NaN m/z “sorts to the end” is false. Smallest fix: reject traces with non-finite m/z, RT, IM, intensity, or XIC values before sorting and inference.
    20	
    21	- **HIGH — cross-frame aggregation can reuse already-consumed peaks with negative intensity.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:334`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:334>), `aggregateFrames_()`: consumed peaks are set to `-1`, but later clusters do not skip `buf[b].in < 0`. A peak within tolerance of two nearby IM anchors can be accumulated twice, the second time negatively. Smallest fix: add `buf[b].in > 0` to the membership condition or maintain a separate consumed bitmap.
    22	
    23	- **HIGH — IM units are assumed, not enforced.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1796`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1796>): mzML is accepted after detecting “some IM,” but milliseconds, CCS, and FAIMS values can then be treated numerically as 1/K0. That silently corrupts every IM gate. Smallest fix: verify VSSC/1/K0 units on every relevant spectrum and reject unsupported units with a conversion-oriented error.
    24	
    25	- **HIGH — default banding is asserted to be exact without an exact boundary argument.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:915`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:915>): the claim that a trace cannot span more than one ppm tolerance ignores trace chaining across successive observations. A fixed 20× halo is plausible, not proof. Boundary traces can therefore be truncated or duplicated relative to `bands=1`. Smallest fix: add an equivalence test containing a deliberately drifting boundary trace; if equivalence fails, extend bands until no retained trace touches a halo boundary or assign seeds to cores while allowing unrestricted extension.
    26	
    27	- **HIGH — parameter ranges permit silent nonsense.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1710`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1710>): negative fragment counts are cast to unsigned `Size`; negative tolerances quietly suppress output; negative `mem_fraction` is converted to `size_t`; `SPEXTRACTOR_PICK_BATCH=garbage` becomes zero and `-1` becomes a huge size. Smallest fix: define TOPP min/max constraints and explicitly validate all environment values with checked parsing.
    28	
    29	- **MEDIUM — CSR indexing narrows real input sizes to 32 bits.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:273`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:273>) and [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:725`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:725>): `FragGrid::off` is `uint32_t`, and grid positions are `int`. A sufficiently large window wraps offsets and silently reads the wrong fragment support. Given this tool already operates near hundred-gigabyte scale, this is not academic. Smallest fix: use `size_t` for offsets and indices, or abort before exceeding the chosen representation.
    30	
    31	- **MEDIUM — canonical MS1 ordering is not actually total.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1998`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1998>): two traces with equal m/z, RT, IM, and intensity but different XICs compare equivalent. Their inherited nondeterministic order then changes the index tie-break and greedy `used[]` ownership. Smallest fix: retain a stable physical origin ID or lexicographically compare the XIC as the final tie-break.
    32	
    33	- **MEDIUM — even aggregation sizes do not mean what the parameter says.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:300`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:300>): `n_frames=4` gives `half=2`, hence a five-frame window. Smallest fix: reject even values or implement asymmetric bounds totaling exactly `n_frames`.
    34	
    35	- **MEDIUM — mzPeak array mismatches are normalized into partial input.** [`MzPeakStreamLoad.h:190`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/MzPeakStreamLoad.h:190>): m/z and intensity are truncated with `min()`, while short IM arrays become “no IM.” Smallest fix: require equal array lengths and throw with frame ID and observed sizes.
    36	
    37	I found no confirmed C++ data race in the reviewed OpenMP window loop or MS1 pick loop: picker instances are `firstprivate`, spectra/slots are disjoint, `pdense` is copied per inner worker, and shared appends/errors are protected. I also found no unchecked `--iterator` decrement; the only `prev(it)` at line 1442 is guarded. Empty `lower_bound`/`upper_bound` calls themselves are safe. The remaining risks are deterministic algorithmic wrongness and incomplete total ordering, not an obvious unprotected write.
    38	
    39	## 2. Code versus documentation consistency
    40	
    41	- **BLOCKER — “open-search safe” is not safe and its promised correction metadata does not exist.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:774`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:774>) versus [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1647`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1647>): the flag only unsets guessed charges. Isotope-supported charges remain annotated despite the README admitting only 74.6% agreement. `spx_n_isotopes` is an evidence count, not an isotope-offset hypothesis capable of correcting ±1.00335 Da. Smallest fix: rename the option to `unset_guessed_charge`, remove the safety/correction claim, or implement an actual confidence/offset representation and unset every insufficiently supported charge.
    42	
    43	- **HIGH — the documented “current defaults” are false.** [`dataset D-BASELINE.md:20`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/docs/dataset D-BASELINE.md:20>) says `ms2_noise_threshold_int=10` and `ms2_split_valleys=7`; code defaults are 100 and 0 at [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:780`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:780>) and line 788. Thus the README’s headline numbers are not reproducible from its one-line default invocation. Smallest fix: either change the defaults or label the benchmark overrides explicitly in README and provide the exact INI.
    44	
    45	- **HIGH — several documented experimental options do not exist.** [`CHANGELOG.md:13`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/CHANGELOG.md:13>) claims `SPEXTRACTOR_ISO_COLLAPSE` was added; [`CHANGELOG.md:55`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/CHANGELOG.md:55>) and [`dataset D-BASELINE.md:594`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/docs/dataset D-BASELINE.md:594>) document `SPEXTRACTOR_BACKFILL_RAW`, `BACKFILL_MAXFRAGS`, `BACKFILL_EMPTY`, and `BACKFILL_MAXQ`. None appears in either shipped source file. Smallest fix: restore the implementations or move them into an explicitly historical-results section saying “not present in v0.3.”
    46	
    47	- **HIGH — `trace:split_valleys_fwhm` describes the wrong code path.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:791`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:791>) says it overrides `trace:ms1_split_valleys`; lines 1969–1976 modify only `ms2_split`. Smallest fix: change the help to “overrides `trace:ms2_split_valleys`,” or implement the claimed MS1 two-pass behavior.
    48	
    49	- **HIGH — `charge:ambiguity_margin` is inert under the shipping scoring mode.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:851`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:851>) promises an alternative charge, but the `count` branch exits at line 1246 before the ambiguity block at line 1345. README presents four `count`-mode tests as a falsification; `dataset D-BASELINE.md:100` correctly admits it was unreachable. Smallest fix: mark it “envelope mode only” everywhere or calculate count-mode hypotheses without the early `continue`.
    50	
    51	- **HIGH — benchmark numbers in `--help` are configuration-dependent but presented as properties of the option.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:837`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:837>) embeds dataset D emission, peptide, engine, and runtime numbers produced under non-default MS2 settings. The current default invocation cannot reproduce them. Smallest fix: reduce help to behavior/default/risk and link to a versioned benchmark document containing the complete command and input identity.
    52	
    53	- **MEDIUM — `charge:min_charge` has the correct position but not the claimed guessed-precursor semantics.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2032`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2032>) correctly applies default charge, then isotope-support gate, then minimum-charge filtering before extraction. True charge 0 survives. But a guessed precursor assigned `assembly:default_charge=1` is silently dropped by `min_charge=2`. Smallest fix: filter only `!pc.guessed && pc.charge > 0 && pc.charge < zmin`, or apply the charge gate before default-charge assignment.
    54	
    55	- **MEDIUM — the README contains mutually incompatible reproducibility stories.** [`README.md:83`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/README.md:83>) says spectrum data is byte-identical at any thread count; [`README.md:118`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/README.md:118>) calls four differing counts “runs of an identical effective configuration.” The latter numbers are the ambiguity-margin experiment, not a current converter replicate guarantee. Smallest fix: separate converter determinism, search-engine determinism, and historical same-binary variability; do not reuse treatment-arm counts as replicate variance.
    56	
    57	- **MEDIUM — README runtime is stale within the same document.** [`README.md:69`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/README.md:69>) reports 12:49 for the current charge-gated arm, while line 91 calls 15:21 the current dataset D runtime without saying it is pre-charge-gate. Smallest fix: label 15:21 as the predecessor or delete it.
    58	
    59	- **MEDIUM — “74.6% with current defaults” is denominator-ambiguous after `min_charge=2`.** [`README.md:101`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/README.md:101>): 74.6% was measured before the new filter removes z=1 hypotheses. It is not clearly the agreement of the spectra now emitted by default. Smallest fix: rerun on the post-filter output and state the denominator, or label 74.6% as pre-filter inference accuracy.
    60	
    61	The registered falsified options do exist with defensible off defaults: `competitive` false, `apportion=0`, `rp_max=0`, frame aggregation `=1`, `variance_support` false, `consolidate:delta_rt=0`, `merge:rt_window=0`, `mono_averagine_guard=0`, and `mono_averagine_select` false. The exception is `ambiguity_margin`: it exists and defaults to zero, but its advertised count-mode experiment never exercised the implementation.
    62	
    63	## 3. Is the package complete and usable by someone else?
    64	
    65	- **BLOCKER — the published benchmark is not externally reproducible.** README’s headline refers to dataset A/dataset B/dataset D, but no accession or downloadable raw/reference data is supplied. The harness uses private `/scratch` and `/ceph` paths and private the reference implementation products. Smallest fix: publish a public-data recipe or explicitly label the table “internally reproducible only”; include immutable input/config/binary hashes and a portable harness plan.
    66	
    67	- **HIGH — the build instructions are internally wrong.** [`README.md:18`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/README.md:18>) says to “rebuild the OpenMS DIAPseudoSpectra target,” although README and CMake insist this is standalone. No actual standalone `cmake -S/-B`, build, install, or runtime-library command is given. Smallest fix: provide a complete copy-paste build from fresh OpenMS checkout through `cmake --build` and one verified invocation.
    68	
    69	- **HIGH — dependency identity is not reproducible.** [`README.md:30`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/README.md:30>) names a bespoke `3.6.0-pre-exported-20260717`, while mzPeak is described as fork “trunk.” No OpenMS or mzPeak commit SHA, compiler/standard-library matrix, patch-base SHA, Arrow/Parquet/libzip versions, or container/lockfile is supplied. Smallest fix: pin commits and versions in a release manifest and CI-build exactly that manifest.
    70	
    71	- **HIGH — CMake’s no-OpenMP promise is false.** [`CMakeLists.txt:23`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/CMakeLists.txt:23>) claims a serial build works, but [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:531`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:531>) calls `omp_set_schedule` and uses OpenMP enums outside `_OPENMP`. Smallest fix: guard that call and provide a serial schedule fallback, then CI-build with OpenMP disabled.
    72	
    73	- **HIGH — no realistic resource requirements are given.** The README recommends tcmalloc but the benchmark itself reports 100–156 GB peak RAM. The admission code deliberately admits one oversized window, so a normal workstation can still OOM. Smallest fix: document tested RAM/disk/core requirements, a low-memory command, expected slowdown, and the meaning/limits of `perf:mem_fraction`.
    74	
    75	- **HIGH — there is no worked example with an expected result.** [`README.md:44`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/README.md:44>) gives only a command. There is no public miniature input, expected spectrum count, expected precursor/fragment values, provenance value, or validation command. Smallest fix: ship a small legally redistributable fixture plus expected semantic digest and five representative spectra.
    76	
    77	- **MEDIUM — the input contract is incomplete.** README does not specify that mzML must contain usable MS1 and MS2, per-peak VSSC IM arrays, isolation-window offsets, and suitable profile/centroid metadata. It also does not describe behavior for unsupported IM units or multiple precursor records. Smallest fix: add an “Input contract” table and a preflight command.
    78	
    79	- **MEDIUM — the output contract is incomplete.** “Searchable mzML” does not document charge-0 behavior, `spx_guessed`, `spx_n_isotopes`, calibration provenance, isolation offsets, or which search engines honor an unset precursor charge. Smallest fix: document emitted fields and provide Sage/MSFragger configs known to interpret them correctly.
    80	
    81	- **MEDIUM — third-party attribution is incomplete for optional binary distribution.** [`NOTICE:4`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/NOTICE:4>) covers OpenMS and algorithmic references, but the optional build statically links mzPeak, Arrow, and Parquet and also uses libzip, zlib, and SQLiteCpp. Smallest fix: list these dependencies, licenses, source locations, and any notices required when distributing binaries. The BSD-3 `LICENSE` and OpenMS attribution themselves are present.
    82	
    83	- **MEDIUM — release metadata still says v0.2.0.** [`CMakeLists.txt:2`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/CMakeLists.txt:2>) remains `VERSION 0.2.0`; `CITATION.cff` also remains 0.2.0. Smallest fix: update them atomically at release and add a version-output regression test.
    84	
    85	The one-large-file convention is not itself a usability defect. The absence of a stable, testable and documented release envelope is.
    86	
    87	## 4. Internal development commentary
    88	
    89	My position: retain local engineering rationale, preserve falsified experiments in `docs/`, and remove lab-notebook prose from `--help` and executable hot paths.
    90	
    91	Keep:
    92	
    93	- [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1806`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1806>): why `PeakPickerIM` must be `firstprivate` and exceptions cannot escape OpenMP.
    94	- [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:367`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:367>): compact representation, quantization, and rejection policy.
    95	- [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1988`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1988>): the fact that greedy ownership requires canonical trace ordering—after fixing the incomplete tie-break.
    96	- [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1479`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1479>): why overlap support is checked before correlation.
    97	- [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2833`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2833>): why calibration and weighting provenance must be written.
    98	
    99	Move to `docs/experiments/`:
   100	
   101	- Route/arm chronology, sample counts, dates, reviewer names, and measured peptide deltas at lines 768–856, 998–1005, 1145–1202, 2045–2072, 2192–2252, and 2398–2467.
   102	- `[route-1]`, `[content 3]`, `[step-02]`, `[kimi]`, `[codex]`, dated launch details, and “first attempt/reverted” narratives. These are useful evidence, but they make local invariants indistinguishable from expired experimental context.
   103	- The complete dataset D/dataset A/dataset B outcome histories already belong in `dataset D-BASELINE.md`; source comments should link to a stable experiment ID rather than reproduce the notebook.
   104	
   105	Cut from user-facing help:
   106	
   107	- Per-sample counts, dates, review arguments, “try this next,” and multi-paragraph causal narratives.
   108	- Keep `FALSIFIED` because that is an intentional project convention, but compress it to: “Experimental; default off. Lost X under experiment E-ID; see docs/…”. Help must explain behavior, units, valid range, mode dependencies, and default—not litigate the experiment.
   109	
   110	Factually stale commentary:
   111	
   112	- [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:758`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:758>) calls defaults placeholders pending a real-data sweep. The file then cites months of sweeps.
   113	- [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:244`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:244>) says default is the OpenMS centroid; line 249 defaults to apex.
   114	- [`MzPeakStreamLoad.h:23`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/MzPeakStreamLoad.h:23>) says exact m/z is enabled with `SPEXTRACTOR_MZPEAK_EXACT=1`; actual behavior is default-on and `=0` disables it.
   115	- [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1317`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1317>) documents a non-envelope fallback that is dead code because the earlier count branch always `continue`s.
   116	- [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1947`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1947>) refers to obsolete line `:1324`.
   117	- [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2641`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2641>) says merge intensities are summed; the default implementation takes maximum.
   118	- [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:65`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:65>) sends users to top-level `PLAN.md` and `REVIEW.md`, neither of which exists.
   119	- [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2239`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtractor/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2239>), `bands_for`, is unused “kept for reference” code. Reference implementations belong in history or tests, not the release executable.
