    15	- **`trace:mz_estimator` is a real parameter** (default `apex`), replacing an undocumented
    16	  environment variable that silently set every reported precursor and fragment m/z.
    17	
    18	### Added
    19	- Parameter bounds on the numeric options. A negative count used to wrap through `Size`
    20	  (`min_fragments -1` became ~1.8e19 and nothing was emitted) and a negative tolerance gated
    21	  everything out; both failed as an empty but valid-looking result.
    22	- `bench/iso_dup.py`, `bench/iso_collapse.py`, `bench/iso_sim.py`, `bench/iso_loss.py`,
    23	  `bench/iso_guard.py`: the isotope-duplication census, collapse-rule evaluation, content-similarity
    24	  test and loss trace behind the emission findings.
    25	
    26	### Changed
    27	- **`trace:detector` now defaults to `integer`.** Mass-trace detection runs on the instrument's own
    28	  integer axes -- flight-time bin for m/z, frame index for retention time -- following OpenMS
    29	  MassTraceDetection step for step, and never materialises a PeakMap of doubles. On dataset D it is
    30	  faster and uses ~40% less memory than the OpenMS path, and the two agree to about 1% of identified
    31	  peptides in either direction (Sage -1.3% on dataset D but +0.3% on dataset A; MSFragger +2.1%; entrapment
    32	  slightly lower). Without the vendor flight-time calibration the tool **falls back to `openms` and
    33	  says so**; the emitted mzML records which detector ran as `spx:detector`.
    34	
    35	### Performance (2026-09-04, trace representation)
    36	- **A trace's profile is a span, not a point list.** A trace point has one degree of freedom -- its
    37	  intensity -- so the three parallel 4-byte arrays (frame, intensity, flight-time bin) and their
    38	  three allocations per trace are replaced by an entry frame and a range in a per-window arena, with
    39	  zero meaning a missed frame. Verified **byte-identical on both detectors** on dataset D. The per-window
    40	  correlation grid, which was a second copy of every profile, is gone. Window-loop memory falls from
    41	  a 68.6 GB simultaneous peak to 27.9 GB (integer) / 32.4 GB (OpenMS); process peak RSS 105 -> 88 GB
    42	  and 164 -> 147 GB.
    43	- **Memory ledger corrections** (`SPEXTRACTOR_MEM_LEDGER=1`): the trace line was cumulative rather
    44	  than a peak, and a 6.0 GB `list<Peak2D>` line was a phantom (that list is per trace and dies each
    45	  iteration) now replaced by the two `MassTrace` payloads that really coexist. The visited flags are
    46	  a bitset (4.87 -> 0.61 GB), and the slab and per-window preparation arrays are released before
    47	  valley splitting instead of being held across it.
    48	
    49	### Performance (2026-09-03, window loop)
    50	- **The scoring gate reads its one field from a parallel array instead of striding the 96-byte
    51	  trace record.** The gate rejects 99.4% of the fragments it visits; the scan was moving ~83 TB per
    52	  dataset D run for 8 bytes of payload per visit. dataset D wall 12:53 -> **7:17**, scoring stage 47% -> 4.8% of
    53	  window time, **peptide set identical** on Sage. Replicated three times.
    54	- **The window loop is one OpenMP task pool over all threads** (master/worker) instead of a rigid
    55	  threads/bands x bands grid; window-loop occupancy 65x -> 81x on 100 threads. The number of windows
    56	  resident is bounded by the free-RAM admission gate, whose per-window estimate was re-calibrated
    57	  (10x -> 16x compact bytes; it had been under-booking by 35-55%).
    58	- **The retention-time axis is a global frame index.** A trace point is (frame index, intensity),
    59	  8 B instead of 16, and aligning two profiles is integer equality instead of a binary search per
    60	  point; the per-window correlation grid is built without collecting or sorting ~10^8 RT values.
    61	- `trace:detector=integer` (EXPERIMENTAL, default `openms`): mass-trace detection on integer arrays
    62	  with the instrument's flight-time bin as the m/z axis, following OpenMS MassTraceDetection step
    63	  for step, calibration re-applied only at export. dataset D: 6:53 wall and 105 GB peak against 7:17 and
    64	  164 GB for the OpenMS path, at −1.4% Sage peptides pending attribution and the both-engines rule.
    65	  Not a default. See docs/INTEGER-TRACING-DESIGN.md and docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/.
    66	- Band-count sensitivity measured: 4/6/12/25 bands move the peptide set by −0.02% to +0.10% and
    67	  differ in ~2% of spectra's content. Banding is an approximation, not exact as the help had
    68	  claimed; the halo is now sized per edge.
    69	
    70	### Fixed
    71	- MS1 traces are filtered for non-finite coordinates before sorting. A NaN made the comparator
    72	  non-transitive, which is undefined behaviour in `sort()` and silently corrupts every later binary
    73	  search. The fragment path already did this; the MS1 path did not.
    74	- `FragGrid` CSR offsets widened to 64-bit. At 8.5 M fragments and ~510 support points each, the
    75	  32-bit offsets were within a factor of two of wrapping, and an overflow there silently corrupts
    80	comparison was biased against the open path -- **this is the removal of a self-inflicted handicap,
